window.WB_BOOK=window.WB_BOOK||{};window.WB_BOOK["week08"]={"id":"week08","n":8,"section":"C","label":"","title":"Light, Reflection, Refraction & Lenses","titleText":"Light, Reflection, Refraction & Lenses","subtitle":"What light is, how it travels, how it bounces, how it bends, and how lenses make images","intro":"Last week you learnt about waves in general, sound and the electromagnetic spectrum. This week we zoom in on one part of that spectrum: visible light. You will learn the old argument about whether light is a particle or a wave, how light travels in straight lines (shadows, eclipses, pinhole camera), the laws of reflection, refraction and Snell's law, total internal reflection and optical fibres, and finally lenses and ray diagrams. Optics questions appear on almost every Paper 01 and very often in Paper 02. Most marks come from three skills: drawing ray diagrams neatly with a ruler, using ⟪n=\\sin i/\\sin r⟫ and ⟪\\sin c=1/n⟫, and using the lens formula. Work through the units in order.","objectives":[{"code":"C4.1","text":"Discuss the rival theories of light (Newton's corpuscular theory and Huygens' wave theory)"},{"code":"C4.2","text":"Describe Young's double slit experiment and explain how it supports the wave theory of light"},{"code":"C4.3","text":"Explain why the diffraction of light is not normally observed"},{"code":"C4.4","text":"Give evidence of the rectilinear propagation of light (shadows, eclipses, pinhole camera)"},{"code":"C4.5","text":"State and investigate the laws of reflection"},{"code":"C4.6","text":"Describe the characteristics of images formed in a plane mirror"},{"code":"C4.7","text":"Give evidence of refraction of light"},{"code":"C4.8","text":"Draw diagrams showing refraction of rays towards and away from the normal"},{"code":"C4.9","text":"Describe the dispersion of white light by a prism to form a spectrum"},{"code":"C4.10","text":"Apply Snell's law: ⟪n=\\sin i/\\sin r⟫; and ⟪n=c/v⟫ (speed of light in vacuum ÷ speed in the medium)"},{"code":"C4.11","text":"Explain the critical angle and total internal reflection"},{"code":"C4.12","text":"Apply the relationship ⟪\\sin c=1/n⟫"},{"code":"C4.13","text":"Describe applications of total internal reflection (optical fibres, prisms in periscopes)"},{"code":"C5.1","text":"Describe the effect of converging and diverging lenses on a parallel beam of light"},{"code":"C5.2","text":"Define principal axis, principal focus, focal length, focal plane and magnification"},{"code":"C5.3","text":"Distinguish between real and virtual images and draw ray diagrams to locate images formed by a converging lens"},{"code":"C5.4","text":"Apply magnification = image height / object height = image distance / object distance (v/u)"},{"code":"C5.5","text":"Determine the focal length of a converging lens (distant object method; ⟪1/f=1/u+1/v⟫)"}],"formulaCard":[{"tex":"i=r","meaning":"Law of reflection (angles from the normal); incident ray, reflected ray and normal in the same plane","units":"°"},{"text":"image distance = object distance","meaning":"Plane mirror: image as far behind as object is in front; virtual, upright, same size, laterally inverted","units":"m or cm"},{"text":"image height / object height = image distance / object distance","meaning":"Pinhole camera (and lenses)","units":"no unit"},{"tex":"n=\\sin i/\\sin r","meaning":"Snell's law (i in air, r in medium)","units":"no unit"},{"tex":"n=c/v","meaning":"Refractive index from speeds; ⟪c=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","units":"no unit"},{"tex":"\\sin c=1/n","meaning":"Critical angle; TIR when denser → less dense AND i > c","units":"°"},{"tex":"m=\\text{ image height }/\\text{ object height }=v/u","meaning":"Magnification","units":"no unit"},{"tex":"1/f=1/u+1/v","meaning":"Lens formula; virtual image → v negative","units":"cm or m (same for all)"},{"tex":"\\lambda \\approx 4\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\ (\\text{violet})\\text{ to }7\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\ (red)","meaning":"Visible light; spectrum R O Y G B I V, red deviated least","units":"m"}],"glossary":[{"term":"Corpuscular theory","def":"Newton's theory that light is a stream of tiny particles (corpuscles)."},{"term":"Wave theory","def":"Huygens' theory that light is a wave."},{"term":"Interference","def":"The adding or cancelling of two overlapping waves to give a pattern of strong and weak regions (bright and dark fringes)."},{"term":"Diffraction","def":"The spreading of a wave through a gap or around an obstacle."},{"term":"Monochromatic","def":"Light of one single colour (one wavelength)."},{"term":"Rectilinear propagation","def":"Travel of light in straight lines."},{"term":"Umbra / penumbra","def":"Region of total shadow / region of partial shadow."},{"term":"Normal","def":"A line at 90° to a surface at the point where a ray strikes it."},{"term":"Virtual image","def":"An image from which rays only appear to come; cannot be formed on a screen."},{"term":"Real image","def":"An image formed where rays actually meet; can be formed on a screen."},{"term":"Lateral inversion","def":"Left and right swapped, as in a plane-mirror image."},{"term":"Refraction","def":"Change in direction of light passing from one medium to another, due to a change in speed."},{"term":"Refractive index (n)","def":"sin i / sin r for light entering a medium from air; also c/v."},{"term":"Dispersion","def":"Splitting of white light into its colours by refraction (e.g. by a prism)."},{"term":"Critical angle (c)","def":"Angle of incidence in the denser medium giving an angle of refraction of 90°."},{"term":"Total internal reflection","def":"Complete reflection of light inside a denser medium when i > c."},{"term":"Optical fibre","def":"Thin glass strand that carries light by repeated total internal reflection."},{"term":"Principal focus (F)","def":"Point where rays parallel to the principal axis converge (or appear to diverge from) after passing through a lens."},{"term":"Focal length (f)","def":"Distance from the optical centre to the principal focus."},{"term":"Magnification","def":"Image height ÷ object height ⟪(=v/u)⟫."}],"lessons":[{"id":"week08.8.1","kind":"unit","unit":"8.1","title":"What is Light? Rival Theories, Young's Experiment and Diffraction","titleText":"What is Light? Rival Theories, Young's Experiment and Diffraction","goals":[{"code":"C4.1","text":"Discuss the rival theories of light (Newton's corpuscular theory and Huygens' wave theory)"},{"code":"C4.2","text":"Describe Young's double slit experiment and explain how it supports the wave theory of light"},{"code":"C4.3","text":"Explain why the diffraction of light is not normally observed"}],"codes":["C4.1","C4.2","C4.3"],"words":[{"term":"Corpuscular theory","def":"Newton's theory that light is a stream of tiny particles (corpuscles)."},{"term":"Wave theory","def":"Huygens' theory that light is a wave."},{"term":"Interference","def":"The adding or cancelling of two overlapping waves to give a pattern of strong and weak regions (bright and dark fringes)."},{"term":"Diffraction","def":"The spreading of a wave through a gap or around an obstacle."},{"term":"Monochromatic","def":"Light of one single colour (one wavelength)."},{"term":"Rectilinear propagation","def":"Travel of light in straight lines."},{"term":"Normal","def":"A line at 90° to a surface at the point where a ray strikes it."},{"term":"Refraction","def":"Change in direction of light passing from one medium to another, due to a change in speed."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"h","text":"Two rival theories"},{"type":"p","text":"For about 100 years scientists argued about what light really is. Two great scientists gave two different answers."},{"type":"table","headers":["","Newton — corpuscular (particle) theory","Huygens — wave theory"],"rows":[["Date","about 1670s–1704 (Opticks)","about 1678–1690"],["What is light?","a stream of tiny, fast particles called **corpuscles**","a **wave** spreading out from the source; every point on a wavefront acts as a new source of small wavelets"],["Straight lines / sharp shadows","Explained easily: particles travel in straight lines","Harder to explain — waves should bend round corners (Newton's main objection)"],["Reflection","Explained: particles bounce off like a ball off a wall","Explained: wavefronts reflect"],["Refraction","Predicted light goes **FASTER** in water/glass (pulled towards the surface)","Predicted light goes **SLOWER** in water/glass"],["Interference & diffraction","Could NOT explain","Explained naturally"]]},{"type":"p","text":"Newton was so famous that most scientists accepted the particle theory for over a century. Two pieces of evidence later settled the argument in favour of waves:"},{"type":"bullets","items":["**Thomas Young (1801)** showed that light produces an **interference pattern** — something only waves can do.","**Foucault (1850)** measured the speed of light in water and found it **slower** than in air — exactly what the wave theory predicted and the opposite of Newton's prediction."]},{"type":"remember","text":"Today physicists say light has a **dual nature**: it travels as a wave (interference, diffraction) but can also behave like particles called **photons** (Einstein, photoelectric effect, 1905). For CSEC, know Newton = particles, Huygens = waves, and Young's experiment supports waves."},{"type":"tip","text":"A \"discuss the rival theories\" question (3–4 marks) wants: (1) Newton — light is particles/corpuscles; (2) Huygens — light is a wave; (3) one thing each explained or failed to explain; (4) the evidence that settled it (Young's interference / light slower in water)."},{"type":"h","text":"Young's double slit experiment"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week08/young","alt":"Figure 8.1 — Young's double slit experiment","caption":"Figure 8.1 — Young's double slit experiment","width":5.6,"w":1106,"h":574},{"type":"p","text":"**Method**"},{"type":"steps","items":["Light of ONE colour (**monochromatic**, e.g. from a sodium lamp or a laser) passes through a **single narrow slit**. This makes the light from the two slits come from the same source, so the two sets of waves stay in step (they are **coherent**).","The light then falls on **two very narrow slits S~1~ and S~2~** very close together (less than 1 mm apart).","Light **diffracts** (spreads out) from each slit, and the two sets of waves **overlap**.","A screen is placed about 1 m away. The room is darkened."]},{"type":"p","text":"**Observation:** a pattern of equally spaced **bright and dark bands (fringes)** appears on the screen."},{"type":"p","text":"**Explanation:**"},{"type":"bullets","items":["Where a crest from S~1~ meets a crest from S~2~ (waves in step), they add up → **constructive interference** → a **bright fringe**.","Where a crest from one slit meets a trough from the other (waves out of step), they cancel → **destructive interference** → a **dark fringe**.","Light + light = darkness can ONLY be explained if light is a wave. Particles cannot cancel each other out."]},{"type":"p","text":"**Conclusion:** Young's experiment showed that light shows **interference**, so light behaves as a **wave**. This supports Huygens' wave theory."},{"type":"warn","text":"Do not say the dark fringes are \"shadows\". They are places where waves from the two slits arrive out of step and cancel (destructive interference)."},{"type":"h","text":"Why don't we normally see light diffract?"},{"type":"def","term":"Diffraction","text":"the spreading out of a wave as it passes through a gap or around the edge of an obstacle."},{"type":"p","text":"Diffraction is only noticeable when the size of the gap is **about the same size as the wavelength**."},{"type":"table","headers":["Wave","Typical wavelength","Typical gap (door/window)","Diffraction?"],"rows":[["Sound","about 1 m","about 1 m","YES — you hear round corners"],["Light","about ⟪5\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\ (0.0005\\,\\mathrm{mm})⟫","about 1 m","NO — far too small to notice; sharp shadows"]]},{"type":"p","text":"The wavelength of light is extremely small (about two million times smaller than a door). So light passing through everyday gaps spreads out by a tiny amount we cannot see, and objects cast **sharp shadows**. To see light diffract you need a very narrow slit (about the width of a hair or smaller), as in Young's experiment."},{"type":"tip","text":"Exam answer (2 marks): \"The wavelength of light is very small ⟪(\\approx 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m})⟫ (1) compared with the size of everyday gaps and obstacles, so the amount of diffraction is too small to be noticed (1).\""}],"examples":[{"id":"week08.8.1.e1","title":"Why light does not diffract through a doorway","question":"A door is 0.90 m wide. Sound of frequency 340 Hz and light of frequency ⟪6.0\\times 10^{14}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫ pass through it. Speed of sound ⟪=340\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫. Explain why the sound is heard round the corner but the light is not seen round the corner.","steps":[{"label":"Wavelength of sound","text":"⟪\\lambda =v\\div f=340\\div 340=1.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"},{"label":"Wavelength of light","text":"⟪\\lambda =c\\div f=(3.0\\times 10^{8})\\div (6.0\\times 10^{14})=5.0\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"},{"label":"Compare with gap","text":"Sound: ⟪\\lambda \\ (1.0\\,\\mathrm{m})\\approx gap\\ (0.90\\,\\mathrm{m})⟫ → lots of diffraction. Light: λ is millions of times smaller than the gap → hardly any diffraction."}],"answer":"Sound ⟪\\lambda =1.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ is about the size of the door, so it diffracts a lot; light ⟪\\lambda =5.0\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ is far smaller, so its diffraction is too small to notice."},{"id":"week08.8.1.e2","title":"Explaining Young's fringes","question":"In Young's experiment, explain why a dark fringe forms at some points on the screen.","steps":[{"label":"Two sources","text":"Light from S~1~ and S~2~ overlaps on the screen."},{"label":"Out of step","text":"At a dark fringe, a crest from one slit arrives at the same time as a trough from the other."},{"label":"Cancel","text":"The waves cancel — destructive interference — so no light."}],"answer":"Waves from the two slits arrive out of phase (crest meets trough) and cancel by destructive interference."},{"id":"week08.8.1.e3","title":"Settling the argument","question":"Newton and Huygens made different predictions about the speed of light in water. State each prediction and explain which was shown to be correct.","steps":[{"label":"Newton","text":"Light particles are attracted towards water, so they speed up: light is FASTER in water."},{"label":"Huygens","text":"Waves slow down in a denser medium: light is SLOWER in water."},{"label":"Evidence","text":"Foucault (1850) measured light to be slower in water."}],"answer":"Huygens' prediction (slower in water) was correct, supporting the wave theory."}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week08.8.1.p1","kind":"short","q":"State the name given by Newton to the particles he thought light was made of.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"Corpuscles (1).","points":[{"text":"Corpuscles","marks":1}],"accept":["Corpuscles"],"section":"try"},{"id":"week08.8.1.p2","kind":"self","q":"State TWO properties of light that Newton's corpuscular theory could explain.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Rectilinear propagation / sharp shadows (1); reflection (1). (Also accept refraction, though with the wrong speed prediction.)","points":[{"text":"Rectilinear propagation / sharp shadows","marks":1},{"text":"reflection (Also accept refraction, though with the wrong speed prediction.)","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Settling the argument). Start like this: Newton: Light particles are attracted towards water, so they speed up: light is FASTER in water.","from":3}}],"practice":[{"id":"week08.8.1.p3","kind":"self","q":"State ONE observation that the wave theory could explain but the corpuscular theory could not.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"Interference (Young's fringes) / diffraction (1).","points":[{"text":"Interference (Young's fringes) / diffraction","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Settling the argument). Start like this: Newton: Light particles are attracted towards water, so they speed up: light is FASTER in water.","from":3}},{"id":"week08.8.1.p4","kind":"self","q":"Why must the light in Young's experiment first pass through a single slit (or come from a laser)?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"So that the light reaching both slits comes from one source (1), making the two sets of waves coherent / in step / with a constant phase difference (1).","points":[{"text":"So that the light reaching both slits comes from one source","marks":1},{"text":"making the two sets of waves coherent / in step / with a constant phase difference","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Why light does not diffract through a doorway). Start like this: Wavelength of sound: ⟪\\lambda =v\\div f=340\\div 340=1.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week08.8.1.p5","kind":"self","q":"Describe what is seen on the screen in Young's double slit experiment.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Alternate bright and dark bands/fringes (1), equally spaced (1).","points":[{"text":"Alternate bright and dark bands/fringes","marks":1},{"text":"equally spaced","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Explaining Young's fringes). Start like this: Two sources: Light from S~1~ and S~2~ overlaps on the screen.","from":2}},{"id":"week08.8.1.p6","kind":"self","q":"Explain how bright fringes are formed in Young's experiment.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Waves from the two slits arrive in phase / crest meets crest (1); they add — constructive interference — giving a bright fringe (1).","points":[{"text":"Waves from the two slits arrive in phase / crest meets crest","marks":1},{"text":"they add — constructive interference — giving a bright fringe","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Explaining Young's fringes). Start like this: Two sources: Light from S~1~ and S~2~ overlaps on the screen.","from":2}}],"exit":[{"id":"week08.8.1.p7","kind":"self","q":"Visible light has a wavelength of about ⟪5\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫. Explain why an object in sunlight casts a sharp shadow even though light is a wave.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Wavelength of light is very small compared with the size of the object (1), so diffraction (bending round the edge) is negligible / too small to see (1).","points":[{"text":"Wavelength of light is very small compared with the size of the object","marks":1},{"text":"so diffraction (bending round the edge) is negligible / too small to see","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week08.8.1.p8","kind":"num","q":"Calculate the wavelength of red light of frequency ⟪4.3\\times 10^{14}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫. ⟪(c=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1})⟫","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪\\lambda =c/f=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\div 4.3\\times 10^{14}⟫ (1) ⟪=7.0\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪\\lambda =c/f=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\div 4.3\\times 10^{14}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=7.0\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":7e-7,"sf":2,"unit":"m","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}"},"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week08.8.1.w1","kind":"self","q":"Define a longitudinal wave and give one example.","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"Particles vibrate parallel to the direction of travel; e.g. sound.","points":[{"text":"Particles vibrate parallel to the direction of travel; e.g. sound.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week07.r1"},{"id":"week08.8.1.w2","kind":"num","q":"A wave has ⟪f=50\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫ and ⟪\\lambda =4.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫. Calculate its speed.","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"⟪v=f\\lambda =200\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫.","points":[{"text":"⟪v=f\\lambda =200\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫.","marks":1}],"num":{"value":200,"sf":1,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"section":"warmup","src":"week07.r2"},{"id":"week08.8.1.w3","kind":"short","q":"What property of a sound wave determines its pitch?","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"Frequency.","points":[{"text":"Frequency.","marks":1}],"accept":["Frequency"],"section":"warmup","src":"week07.r3"},{"id":"week08.8.1.w4","kind":"num","q":"An echo is heard 2.0 s after a shout ⟪(v=330\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1})⟫. How far away is the wall?","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"⟪d=vt/2=330\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫.","points":[{"text":"⟪d=vt/2=330\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫.","marks":1}],"num":{"value":330,"sf":2,"unit":"m","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}"},"section":"warmup","src":"week07.r4"},{"id":"week08.8.1.w5","kind":"self","q":"List the EM spectrum from longest to shortest wavelength.","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"Radio, microwaves, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-rays, gamma rays.","points":[{"text":"Radio, microwaves, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-rays, gamma rays.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week07.r5"}],"quickStart":[{"type":"p","text":"Before Unit 8.1, check these four tools. All of this week's calculations use them."},{"type":"h","text":"1. Using sin and sin⁻¹ on your calculator"},{"type":"p","text":"Make sure your calculator is in **DEG** (degrees) mode — look for a small D at the top of the screen. Test: sin 30 should give **0.5**. If it gives −0.988, you are in radians. Fix this before every exam."},{"type":"bullets","items":["⟪\\sin 40^{\\circ}=0.643⟫","To find an angle from its sine, press **SHIFT sin** (sin⁻¹): ⟪\\sin ^{-1}(0.5)=30^{\\circ}⟫.","sin of any angle from 0° to 90° is between 0 and 1. If you get sin r bigger than 1, you have made an error (or the ray is totally internally reflected!)."]},{"type":"h","text":"2. Angles are measured from the NORMAL"},{"type":"p","text":"The **normal** is a line drawn at 90° to a surface at the point where the ray hits it. In optics, angles i and r are ALWAYS measured between the ray and the normal — never between the ray and the surface. If a question gives the angle to the surface, subtract from 90°."},{"type":"h","text":"3. Fractions and reciprocals"},{"type":"p","text":"For the lens formula you need reciprocals: ⟪1/10+1/15=0.1+0.0667=0.1667⟫, then ⟪1\\div 0.1667=6.0⟫. On a calculator use the **x⁻¹** button."},{"type":"h","text":"4. Recap from Week 7"},{"type":"bullets","items":["Light is an **electromagnetic wave**: transverse, travels at ⟪c=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ in a vacuum, needs no medium.","⟪v=f\\lambda⟫ applies to light too. Visible light has ⟪\\lambda \\approx 4\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (violet) to ⟪7\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\ (red)⟫."]}],"n":55},{"id":"week08.8.2","kind":"unit","unit":"8.2","title":"Light Travels in Straight Lines: Shadows, Eclipses and the Pinhole Camera","titleText":"Light Travels in Straight Lines: Shadows, Eclipses and the Pinhole Camera","goals":[{"code":"C4.4","text":"Give evidence of the rectilinear propagation of light (shadows, eclipses, pinhole camera)"}],"codes":["C4.4"],"words":[{"term":"Rectilinear propagation","def":"Travel of light in straight lines."},{"term":"Magnification","def":"Image height ÷ object height ⟪(=v/u)⟫."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"def","term":"Rectilinear propagation","text":"light travels in straight lines in a uniform medium."},{"type":"p","text":"We draw light as **rays** — straight lines with arrows showing the direction. A **beam** is a bundle of rays. Simple evidence:"},{"type":"bullets","items":["You cannot see round a corner.","Sunlight through gaps in clouds or trees forms straight beams.","**Experiment:** line up three cards with small holes in a row and look at a candle through them. You see the flame only when the holes are in a **straight line** (check with a string pulled tight through the holes). Move one card sideways and the flame disappears."]},{"type":"h","text":"Shadows"},{"type":"p","text":"A **shadow** forms because light travelling in straight lines cannot pass through an **opaque** object."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week08/shadows","alt":"Figure 8.2 — Shadows from a point source (left) and an extended source (right)","caption":"Figure 8.2 — Shadows from a point source (left) and an extended source (right)","width":6,"w":1397,"h":466},{"type":"def","term":"Umbra","text":"the region of total shadow, where NO light from the source reaches."},{"type":"def","term":"Penumbra","text":"the region of partial shadow, where light from only PART of the source reaches."},{"type":"bullets","items":["A **point source** (very small) gives a sharp shadow with **umbra only**.","An **extended source** (large, e.g. a fluorescent tube or the Sun) gives a dark **umbra** surrounded by a lighter **penumbra**."]},{"type":"h","text":"Eclipses"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week08/eclipses","alt":"Figure 8.3 — Solar eclipse (top) and lunar eclipse (bottom)","caption":"Figure 8.3 — Solar eclipse (top) and lunar eclipse (bottom)","width":5.4,"w":1177,"h":753},{"type":"table","headers":["","Solar eclipse (eclipse of the Sun)","Lunar eclipse (eclipse of the Moon)"],"rows":[["Order","Sun — **Moon** — Earth","Sun — **Earth** — Moon"],["What happens","The Moon's shadow falls on part of the Earth","The Moon moves into the Earth's shadow"],["Seen as","Total eclipse from the umbra; partial eclipse from the penumbra","The Moon goes dark (often reddish)"]]},{"type":"tip","text":"Remember the order with: the object in the MIDDLE is the one casting the shadow. In a solar eclipse the Moon is in the middle and blocks the Sun."},{"type":"h","text":"The pinhole camera"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week08/pinhole","alt":"Figure 8.4 — The pinhole camera: rays from the top and bottom of the object cross at the pinhole","caption":"Figure 8.4 — The pinhole camera: rays from the top and bottom of the object cross at the pinhole","width":5,"w":1174,"h":484},{"type":"p","text":"A pinhole camera is a light-tight box with a tiny hole at the front and a tracing-paper screen at the back. Rays from the top of the object travel in a straight line through the hole to the bottom of the screen, and rays from the bottom go to the top. So the image is:"},{"type":"bullets","items":["**real** (formed on a screen)","**inverted** (upside down) and laterally inverted (left and right swapped)","usually **diminished** (smaller) — depends on distances"]},{"type":"formula","text":"magnification = image height / object height = image distance / object distance","where":["image distance = length of the box (pinhole to screen)","object distance = pinhole to object","both heights in the same unit; both distances in the same unit"],"label":"PINHOLE CAMERA"},{"type":"table","headers":["Change","Effect on image"],"rows":[["Move the object closer / use a longer box","Image becomes larger"],["Make the pinhole larger","Image becomes brighter but blurred (each point gives a patch of light)"],["Make two pinholes","Two overlapping images"]]},{"type":"warn","text":"A pinhole camera has NO lens. Its image is formed only because light travels in straight lines. Do not draw rays bending at the hole."}],"examples":[{"id":"week08.8.2.e1","title":"Pinhole camera image size","question":"A tree 1.8 m tall is 6.0 m from a pinhole camera. The box is 20 cm long. Calculate the height of the image.","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"image height / object height = image distance / object distance"},{"label":"Same units","text":"box length ⟪=20\\,\\mathrm{cm}=0.20\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"image height ⟪=1.8\\times (0.20\\div 6.0)⟫"},{"label":"Calculate","text":"image height ⟪=1.8\\times 0.0333=0.060\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"}],"answer":"Image height ⟪=0.060\\,\\mathrm{m}=6.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫ (inverted)."},{"id":"week08.8.2.e2","title":"Explaining a solar eclipse","question":"Explain, with reference to how light travels, why a total solar eclipse is seen from only a small part of the Earth.","steps":[{"label":"Straight lines","text":"Light from the Sun travels in straight lines, so the Moon casts a shadow."},{"label":"Umbra","text":"The Moon's umbra (total shadow) is a narrow cone that just reaches the Earth."},{"label":"Penumbra","text":"Around it, people in the penumbra see only part of the Sun blocked."}],"answer":"Only places inside the small umbra receive no direct sunlight, so only they see a total eclipse; places in the penumbra see a partial eclipse."}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week08.8.2.p1","kind":"self","q":"What is meant by **rectilinear propagation** of light?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"Light travels in straight lines (1).","points":[{"text":"Light travels in straight lines","marks":1}],"section":"try"},{"id":"week08.8.2.p2","kind":"self","q":"Describe a simple experiment, using three cards with holes, to show that light travels in straight lines.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Three cards with a small hole in each, placed in a row with a candle/lamp behind (1); flame seen only when a string/thread passes straight through all holes, i.e. holes in a straight line (1); move one card sideways and the flame can no longer be seen (1).","points":[{"text":"Three cards with a small hole in each, placed in a row with a candle/lamp behind","marks":1},{"text":"flame seen only when a string/thread passes straight through all holes, i.e. holes in a straight line","marks":1},{"text":"move one card sideways and the flame can no longer be seen","marks":1}],"section":"try"}],"practice":[{"id":"week08.8.2.p3","kind":"self","q":"Distinguish between the **umbra** and the **penumbra**.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Umbra: region of total shadow, no light from the source reaches it (1). Penumbra: partial shadow, light from part of the source reaches it (1).","points":[{"text":"Umbra: region of total shadow, no light from the source reaches it","marks":1},{"text":"Penumbra: partial shadow, light from part of the source reaches it","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week08.8.2.p4","kind":"self","q":"Why does a small torch bulb held close to a ball give a shadow with no penumbra?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"It acts as a point source, so there is no region that receives light from only part of the source (1).","points":[{"text":"It acts as a point source, so there is no region that receives light from only part of the source","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week08.8.2.p5","kind":"self","q":"Draw the positions of the Sun, Earth and Moon during a lunar eclipse.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Sun, Earth, Moon in a straight line (1) with Earth in the middle and Moon in Earth's shadow (1).","points":[{"text":"Sun, Earth, Moon in a straight line","marks":1},{"text":"with Earth in the middle and Moon in Earth's shadow","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Explaining a solar eclipse). Start like this: Straight lines: Light from the Sun travels in straight lines, so the Moon casts a shadow.","from":2}},{"id":"week08.8.2.p6","kind":"self","q":"State THREE characteristics of the image formed by a pinhole camera.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Real (1); inverted (1); diminished/smaller (usually) (1). (Also accept laterally inverted.)","points":[{"text":"Real","marks":1},{"text":"inverted","marks":1},{"text":"diminished/smaller (usually) (Also accept laterally inverted.)","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Pinhole camera image size). Start like this: Formula: image height / object height = image distance / object distance","from":1}}],"exit":[{"id":"week08.8.2.p7","kind":"num","q":"A building 15 m tall is 50 m from a pinhole camera of length 0.10 m. Calculate the height of the image.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"image height = object height × image distance / object distance (1) ⟪=15\\times 0.10/50⟫ (1) ⟪=0.030\\,\\mathrm{m}=3.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"image height = object height × image distance / object distance","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=15\\times 0.10/50⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=0.030\\,\\mathrm{m}=3.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":3,"sf":2,"unit":"cm","unitTex":"\\mathrm{cm}"},"section":"exit"},{"id":"week08.8.2.p8","kind":"self","q":"A student makes the pinhole of her camera larger. State and explain TWO changes in the image.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"Brighter (1) because more light enters (1); more blurred (1) because each point on the object produces a patch of light on the screen, and the patches overlap (1).","points":[{"text":"Brighter","marks":1},{"text":"because more light enters","marks":1},{"text":"more blurred","marks":1},{"text":"because each point on the object produces a patch of light on the screen, and the patches overlap","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week08.8.2.w1","kind":"short","q":"State the name given by Newton to the particles he thought light was made of.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"Corpuscles (1).","points":[{"text":"Corpuscles","marks":1}],"accept":["Corpuscles"],"section":"warmup","src":"week08.8.1.p1"},{"id":"week08.8.2.w2","kind":"self","q":"State ONE observation that the wave theory could explain but the corpuscular theory could not.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"Interference (Young's fringes) / diffraction (1).","points":[{"text":"Interference (Young's fringes) / diffraction","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week08.8.1.p3"}],"n":56},{"id":"week08.8.3","kind":"unit","unit":"8.3","title":"Reflection and Images in a Plane Mirror","titleText":"Reflection and Images in a Plane Mirror","goals":[{"code":"C4.5","text":"State and investigate the laws of reflection"},{"code":"C4.6","text":"Describe the characteristics of images formed in a plane mirror"}],"codes":["C4.5","C4.6"],"words":[{"term":"Normal","def":"A line at 90° to a surface at the point where a ray strikes it."},{"term":"Magnification","def":"Image height ÷ object height ⟪(=v/u)⟫."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"h","text":"Key words"},{"type":"def","term":"Incident ray","text":"the ray of light arriving at the surface."},{"type":"def","term":"Reflected ray","text":"the ray of light leaving the surface after reflection."},{"type":"def","term":"Normal","text":"a line drawn at right angles (90°) to the surface at the point where the ray strikes it."},{"type":"def","term":"Angle of incidence (i)","text":"the angle between the incident ray and the normal."},{"type":"def","term":"Angle of reflection (r)","text":"the angle between the reflected ray and the normal."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week08/reflection_laws","alt":"Figure 8.5 — Reflection at a plane mirror","caption":"Figure 8.5 — Reflection at a plane mirror","width":3.6,"w":833,"h":574},{"type":"h","text":"The laws of reflection"},{"type":"steps","items":["The incident ray, the reflected ray and the normal at the point of incidence all lie in the **same plane**.","The angle of incidence is **equal to** the angle of reflection: ⟪\\boldsymbol{i=r}⟫."]},{"type":"remember","text":"BOTH laws are needed for full marks when asked to \"state the laws of reflection\". Many students forget the first one."},{"type":"h","text":"Experiment: investigating the laws of reflection"},{"type":"p","text":"**Apparatus:** plane mirror, ray box with a single slit (or optical pins), protractor, plain paper, pencil, ruler."},{"type":"steps","items":["Draw a straight line on the paper and stand the mirror's reflecting surface on the line. Draw a normal (at 90°) at point O on the line.","Draw lines from O at angles of incidence 20°, 30°, 40°, 50°, 60° to the normal.","Shine the ray from the ray box along each line so it hits the mirror at O. Mark the reflected ray with two crosses far apart and join them to O. (Pin method: put two pins on the incident line; look into the mirror and place two more pins in line with the images of the first two.)","Measure the angle of reflection r for each i with the protractor.","Record i and r in a table."]},{"type":"p","text":"**Results:** i and r are equal for every reading (within about ±1°). **Conclusion:** angle of incidence = angle of reflection. Because the rays and normal were all traced on the flat paper, they lie in the same plane."},{"type":"p","text":"**Precautions:** use a thin ray / thin pencil lines; place pins far apart (at least 5 cm) and vertical; view the bases of the pins to avoid parallax; draw the normal accurately with a protractor."},{"type":"h","text":"Images in a plane mirror"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week08/plane_mirror","alt":"Figure 8.6 — Locating the image of a point object O in a plane mirror","caption":"Figure 8.6 — Locating the image of a point object O in a plane mirror","width":4.4,"w":1054,"h":648},{"type":"p","text":"**How to draw it:** draw two rays from O to the mirror. Reflect each one so that ⟪i=r⟫. Extend the reflected rays **backwards** behind the mirror with **dashed** lines. Where the dashed lines meet is the image I. The eye sees the light as if it comes from I."},{"type":"table","headers":["Characteristic","Plane-mirror image"],"rows":[["Real or virtual?","**Virtual** — cannot be formed on a screen (no light actually goes behind the mirror)"],["Way up","**Upright** (erect)"],["Size","**Same size** as the object (magnification ⟪=1⟫)"],["Distance","As far **behind** the mirror as the object is in front ⟪(v=u)⟫"],["Left/right","**Laterally inverted** (left and right swapped)"],["Line joining object and image","Perpendicular (90°) to the mirror"]]},{"type":"tip","text":"Learn the five: virtual, upright, same size, same distance behind, laterally inverted. A 3-mark question accepts any three."},{"type":"p","text":"**Uses:** looking glasses, mirror periscopes (two mirrors at 45°), and the word AMBULANCE written backwards on the front of ambulances so drivers see it the right way round in their rear-view mirror."},{"type":"warn","text":"Virtual rays (behind the mirror) and the image must be drawn DASHED. Real rays are solid with arrows. Examiners deduct a mark for solid lines behind a mirror."}],"examples":[{"id":"week08.8.3.e1","title":"Angle given to the surface","question":"A ray strikes a plane mirror making an angle of 30° with the SURFACE of the mirror. Find (a) the angle of incidence, (b) the angle between the incident and reflected rays.","steps":[{"label":"Angle to normal","text":"⟪i=90^{\\circ}-30^{\\circ}=60^{\\circ}⟫"},{"label":"Law of reflection","text":"⟪r=i=60^{\\circ}⟫"},{"label":"Angle between rays","text":"⟪i+r=60^{\\circ}+60^{\\circ}=120^{\\circ}⟫"}],"answer":"(a) ⟪i=60^{\\circ}⟫  (b) 120°"},{"id":"week08.8.3.e2","title":"Distance to image","question":"A girl stands 2.5 m in front of a plane mirror. (a) How far is she from her image? (b) She walks 1.0 m towards the mirror. How far is she now from her image?","steps":[{"label":"Image distance","text":"Image is as far behind as she is in front: 2.5 m behind."},{"label":"(a) Girl to image","text":"⟪2.5+2.5=5.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"},{"label":"(b) New distance","text":"She is ⟪now2.5-1.0=1.5\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ in front, image 1.5 m behind → ⟪1.5+1.5=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"}],"answer":"(a) 5.0 m  (b) 3.0 m"}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week08.8.3.p1","kind":"self","q":"Define the **normal** to a surface.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"A line at right angles (90°) to the surface at the point of incidence (1).","points":[{"text":"A line at right angles (90°) to the surface at the point of incidence","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Angle given to the surface). Start like this: Angle to normal: ⟪i=90^{\\circ}-30^{\\circ}=60^{\\circ}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week08.8.3.p2","kind":"self","q":"State the TWO laws of reflection.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Incident ray, reflected ray and normal all lie in the same plane (1). Angle of incidence = angle of reflection (1).","points":[{"text":"Incident ray, reflected ray and normal all lie in the same plane","marks":1},{"text":"Angle of incidence = angle of reflection","marks":1}],"section":"try"}],"practice":[{"id":"week08.8.3.p3","kind":"self","q":"A ray hits a mirror with an angle of incidence of 35°. What is the angle between the incident ray and the reflected ray?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"⟪35^{\\circ}+35^{\\circ}=70^{\\circ}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪35^{\\circ}+35^{\\circ}=70^{\\circ}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Angle given to the surface). Start like this: Angle to normal: ⟪i=90^{\\circ}-30^{\\circ}=60^{\\circ}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week08.8.3.p4","kind":"self","q":"A ray strikes a mirror at 25° to the mirror surface. State the angle of reflection.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪i=90^{\\circ}-25^{\\circ}=65^{\\circ}⟫ (1); ⟪r=65^{\\circ}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪i=90^{\\circ}-25^{\\circ}=65^{\\circ}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪r=65^{\\circ}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Angle given to the surface). Start like this: Angle to normal: ⟪i=90^{\\circ}-30^{\\circ}=60^{\\circ}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week08.8.3.p5","kind":"self","q":"State FOUR characteristics of the image formed in a plane mirror.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"Any four: virtual (1); upright (1); same size (1); same distance behind the mirror as the object in front (1); laterally inverted (1).","points":[{"text":"Any four: virtual","marks":1},{"text":"upright","marks":1},{"text":"same size","marks":1},{"text":"same distance behind the mirror as the object in front","marks":1},{"text":"laterally inverted","marks":1}],"conf":"low","section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Distance to image). Start like this: Image distance: Image is as far behind as she is in front: 2.5 m behind.","from":2}},{"id":"week08.8.3.p6","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the image in a plane mirror is described as **virtual**.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Rays do not actually pass through the image / only appear to come from it (1); so it cannot be formed/caught on a screen (1).","points":[{"text":"Rays do not actually pass through the image / only appear to come from it","marks":1},{"text":"so it cannot be formed/caught on a screen","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Distance to image). Start like this: Image distance: Image is as far behind as she is in front: 2.5 m behind.","from":2}},{"id":"week08.8.3.p7","kind":"self","q":"A man is 3.0 m from a plane mirror. He moves back 0.5 m. Calculate the new distance between the man and his image.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"New distance from mirror ⟪=3.5\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1); man to image ⟪=2\\times 3.5=7.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"New distance from mirror ⟪=3.5\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"man to image ⟪=2\\times 3.5=7.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Distance to image). Start like this: Image distance: Image is as far behind as she is in front: 2.5 m behind.","from":2}}],"exit":[{"id":"week08.8.3.p8","kind":"self","q":"Describe how you would use optical pins to locate the image of a pin placed in front of a plane mirror.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":5,"scheme":"Place object pin O in front of mirror (1); look into mirror from one side and place two pins in line with the image of O (1); repeat from a different position with two more pins (1); join each pair, extend behind the mirror with dashed lines — the crossing point is the image (1).","points":[{"text":"Place object pin O in front of mirror","marks":1},{"text":"look into mirror from one side and place two pins in line with the image of O","marks":1},{"text":"repeat from a different position with two more pins","marks":1},{"text":"join each pair, extend behind the mirror with dashed lines — the crossing point is the image","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week08.8.3.p9","kind":"self","q":"Why is the word AMBULANCE written as \"ƎƆNA⅃UBMA\" on the front of an ambulance?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Plane-mirror images are laterally inverted (1), so drivers ahead see the word the right way round in their rear-view mirrors (1).","points":[{"text":"Plane-mirror images are laterally inverted","marks":1},{"text":"so drivers ahead see the word the right way round in their rear-view mirrors","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week08.8.3.w1","kind":"self","q":"What is meant by **rectilinear propagation** of light?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"Light travels in straight lines (1).","points":[{"text":"Light travels in straight lines","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week08.8.2.p1"},{"id":"week08.8.3.w2","kind":"self","q":"Why does a small torch bulb held close to a ball give a shadow with no penumbra?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"It acts as a point source, so there is no region that receives light from only part of the source (1).","points":[{"text":"It acts as a point source, so there is no region that receives light from only part of the source","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week08.8.2.p4"}],"n":57},{"id":"week08.8.4","kind":"unit","unit":"8.4","title":"Refraction, Snell's Law and Dispersion","titleText":"Refraction, Snell's Law and Dispersion","goals":[{"code":"C4.7","text":"Give evidence of refraction of light"},{"code":"C4.8","text":"Draw diagrams showing refraction of rays towards and away from the normal"},{"code":"C4.9","text":"Describe the dispersion of white light by a prism to form a spectrum"},{"code":"C4.10","text":"Apply Snell's law: ⟪n=\\sin i/\\sin r⟫; and ⟪n=c/v⟫ (speed of light in vacuum ÷ speed in the medium)"}],"codes":["C4.7","C4.8","C4.9","C4.10"],"words":[{"term":"Normal","def":"A line at 90° to a surface at the point where a ray strikes it."},{"term":"Refraction","def":"Change in direction of light passing from one medium to another, due to a change in speed."},{"term":"Refractive index (n)","def":"sin i / sin r for light entering a medium from air; also c/v."},{"term":"Dispersion","def":"Splitting of white light into its colours by refraction (e.g. by a prism)."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"def","term":"Refraction","text":"the change in direction of light as it passes from one medium into another, caused by a change in its speed."},{"type":"h","text":"Evidence of refraction (everyday)"},{"type":"bullets","items":["A swimming pool or river looks **shallower** than it really is.","A straight pencil or stick put into water at an angle looks **bent** at the surface.","A coin at the bottom of a cup comes into view when water is poured in.","Objects seen through a glass block appear shifted sideways."]},{"type":"img","src":"img/week08/apparent_depth","alt":"Figure 8.7 — Real and apparent depth: rays bend away from the normal as they leave the water","caption":"Figure 8.7 — Real and apparent depth: rays bend away from the normal as they leave the water","width":3.8,"w":894,"h":577},{"type":"h","text":"Towards or away from the normal?"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week08/glass_block","alt":"Figure 8.8 — Refraction through a rectangular glass block","caption":"Figure 8.8 — Refraction through a rectangular glass block","width":4.2,"w":974,"h":734},{"type":"table","headers":["Light goes from…","Speed","Bends…","Angle"],"rows":[["less dense → more dense (air → glass/water)","slows down","**TOWARDS** the normal","r < i"],["more dense → less dense (glass/water → air)","speeds up","**AWAY FROM** the normal","r > i"],["along the normal ⟪(i=0^{\\circ})⟫","changes","does **not** bend","⟪r=0^{\\circ}⟫"]]},{"type":"remember","text":"Memory trick: going INTO glass, light slows down and \"hugs\" the normal (FAST → SLOW → TOWARDS). Coming OUT, it speeds up and moves AWAY. With a rectangular block the emergent ray is **parallel** to the incident ray, just shifted sideways (lateral displacement)."},{"type":"warn","text":"The frequency of light does NOT change when it refracts. Its speed and wavelength change. (⟪v=f\\lambda⟫: v smaller → λ smaller, f the same.)"},{"type":"h","text":"Refractive index and Snell's law"},{"type":"def","term":"Snell's law","text":"for light passing from one medium to another, sin i / sin r is a constant."},{"type":"formula","tex":"n=\\sin i/\\sin r","where":["⟪n⟫ = refractive index (no unit)","⟪i⟫ = angle in air (or vacuum), measured from the normal","⟪r⟫ = angle in the medium, measured from the normal"],"label":"SNELL'S LAW (light going from air into the medium)"},{"type":"formula","tex":"n=c/v=\\text{ speed of light in vacuum }(\\text{air})/\\text{ speed of light in medium}","where":["⟪c⟫ = ⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","⟪v⟫ = speed of light in the medium (m s^−1^)","n is always greater than 1, because light is slower in any medium"],"label":"REFRACTIVE INDEX FROM SPEEDS"},{"type":"table","headers":["Medium","Refractive index n","Speed of light / m s^−1^"],"rows":[["Air (≈ vacuum)","1.00","⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}⟫"],["Water","1.33","⟪2.3\\times 10^{8}⟫"],["Glass","1.5 (1.5–1.6)","⟪2.0\\times 10^{8}⟫"],["Diamond","2.42","⟪1.24\\times 10^{8}⟫"]]},{"type":"tip","text":"A bigger n means light slows down MORE and bends MORE. Diamond bends light the most."},{"type":"h","text":"Experiment: verifying Snell's law and finding n for glass"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week08/snell_pins","alt":"Figure 8.9 — Pin method for tracing a ray through a glass block","caption":"Figure 8.9 — Pin method for tracing a ray through a glass block","width":3.5,"w":748,"h":734},{"type":"steps","items":["Place the glass block on paper and draw round its outline. Remove it and draw a normal N at a point on the top face.","Draw an incident ray at ⟪i=20^{\\circ}⟫ to the normal. Stick two pins P~1~ and P~2~ on this line, far apart.","Replace the block. Look through the opposite face and place pins P~3~ and P~4~ so they are exactly in line with the images of P~1~ and P~2~ (look at the bases of the pins).","Remove the block. Join P~3~P~4~ to meet the bottom face. Join this point to where the incident ray entered — this is the refracted ray. Measure r.","Repeat for ⟪i=30^{\\circ}⟫, 40°, 50°, 60°, 70°.","Calculate sin i and sin r. Plot **sin i (y-axis) against sin r (x-axis)**."]},{"type":"p","text":"**Result:** a straight line through the origin, so ⟪\\sin i\\propto \\sin r⟫ (Snell's law is verified). **Gradient **⟪\\boldsymbol{=n}⟫, the refractive index of the glass."},{"type":"p","text":"**Precautions:** pins vertical and well separated; view the bases of the pins (avoid parallax); keep the block exactly on its outline; use a sharp pencil; use a range of angles."},{"type":"h","text":"Dispersion: the spectrum"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week08/prism","alt":"Figure 8.10 — Dispersion of white light by a glass prism","caption":"Figure 8.10 — Dispersion of white light by a glass prism","width":4.8,"w":923,"h":614},{"type":"def","term":"Dispersion","text":"the splitting of white light into its component colours (a spectrum) because each colour is refracted by a different amount."},{"type":"bullets","items":["White light is a **mixture** of colours. The spectrum order is **R O Y G B I V** — Red, Orange, Yellow, Green, Blue, Indigo, Violet (\"Richard Of York Gave Battle In Vain\").","Each colour travels at a slightly **different speed in glass**: red is slowed the least, violet the most.","So glass has a slightly different refractive index for each colour: **red is deviated (bent) LEAST, violet MOST**.","Red has the longest wavelength ⟪(\\approx 7\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m})⟫ and violet the shortest ⟪(\\approx 4\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m})⟫.","A rainbow is dispersion of sunlight by raindrops. A second prism turned the other way can recombine the colours into white light (Newton's experiment)."]}],"examples":[{"id":"week08.8.4.e1","title":"Refractive index from angles","question":"A ray of light enters a glass block from air with an angle of incidence of 40°. The angle of refraction is 25°. Calculate the refractive index of the glass.","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪n=\\sin i/\\sin r⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪n=\\sin 40^{\\circ}/\\sin 25^{\\circ}=0.643/0.423⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪n=1.52⟫ (no unit)"}],"answer":"⟪n=1.52⟫"},{"id":"week08.8.4.e2","title":"Finding the angle of refraction","question":"Light enters glass of refractive index 1.50 at an angle of incidence of 60°. Calculate the angle of refraction.","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪n=\\sin i/\\sin r\\rightarrow \\sin r=\\sin i/n⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪\\sin r=\\sin 60^{\\circ}/1.50=0.866/1.50=0.577⟫"},{"label":"Inverse sine","text":"⟪r=\\sin ^{-1}(0.577)=35.3^{\\circ}⟫"}],"answer":"⟪r=35^{\\circ}⟫ (to 2 s.f.); the ray bends towards the normal (35° < 60°)."},{"id":"week08.8.4.e3","title":"Speed of light in a medium","question":"The refractive index of water is 1.33. Calculate the speed of light in water.","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪n=c/v\\rightarrow v=c/n⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪v=3.0\\times 10^{8}/1.33⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪v=2.26\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫"}],"answer":"⟪v=2.3\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫"},{"id":"week08.8.4.e4","title":"n from a graph","question":"A student plots sin i against sin r for a glass block (Figure 8.11). Use the graph to find the refractive index.","img":{"src":"img/week08/snell_graph","alt":"A student plots sin i against sin r for a glass block (Figure 8.11). Use the graph to find the refractive index.","w":974,"h":694},"steps":[{"label":"Large triangle","text":"From the line: at ⟪\\sin r=0.10⟫, ⟪\\sin i=0.15⟫; at ⟪\\sin r=0.50⟫, ⟪\\sin i=0.75⟫"},{"label":"Gradient","text":"gradient ⟪=\\Delta (\\sin i)/\\Delta (\\sin r)=(0.75-0.15)/\\ (0.50-0.10)=0.60/0.40⟫"},{"label":"Meaning","text":"gradient ⟪=\\sin i/\\sin r=n⟫"}],"answer":"⟪n=1.5⟫"}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week08.8.4.p1","kind":"self","q":"Define **refraction** of light.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"The change in direction of light (1) as it passes from one medium to another, due to a change in speed (1).","points":[{"text":"The change in direction of light","marks":1},{"text":"as it passes from one medium to another, due to a change in speed","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Refractive index from angles). Start like this: Formula: ⟪n=\\sin i/\\sin r⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week08.8.4.p2","kind":"self","q":"Give TWO everyday observations that are evidence of refraction.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Any two: pool looks shallower than it is (1); stick looks bent in water (1); coin in cup appears when water is added (1).","points":[{"text":"Any two: pool looks shallower than it is","marks":1},{"text":"stick looks bent in water","marks":1},{"text":"coin in cup appears when water is added","marks":1}],"conf":"low","section":"try"}],"practice":[{"id":"week08.8.4.p3","kind":"self","q":"Draw a ray diagram showing a ray of light passing from air, through a rectangular glass block, and out into air again. Label the normals, i and r.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":6,"scheme":"Ray bends towards normal on entry (1); bends away from normal on exit (1); emergent ray parallel to incident ray (1); normals drawn dashed at 90° and angles i, r correctly labelled between ray and normal (1).","points":[{"text":"Ray bends towards normal on entry","marks":1},{"text":"bends away from normal on exit","marks":1},{"text":"emergent ray parallel to incident ray","marks":1},{"text":"normals drawn dashed at 90° and angles i, r correctly labelled between ray and normal","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Refractive index from angles). Start like this: Formula: ⟪n=\\sin i/\\sin r⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week08.8.4.p4","kind":"self","q":"A ray of light travels along the normal into a glass block. What happens to its direction and its speed?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Direction unchanged / not bent (1); speed decreases (1).","points":[{"text":"Direction unchanged / not bent","marks":1},{"text":"speed decreases","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Speed of light in a medium). Start like this: Formula: ⟪n=c/v\\rightarrow v=c/n⟫","from":3}},{"id":"week08.8.4.p5","kind":"num","q":"Light enters a liquid from air. ⟪i=50^{\\circ}⟫, ⟪r=32^{\\circ}⟫. Calculate the refractive index of the liquid.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪n=\\sin 50^{\\circ}/\\sin 32^{\\circ}=0.766/0.530⟫ (1) ⟪=1.45⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪n=\\sin 50^{\\circ}/\\sin 32^{\\circ}=0.766/0.530⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=1.45⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":1.45,"sf":3,"unit":"","unitTex":""},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Refractive index from angles). Start like this: Formula: ⟪n=\\sin i/\\sin r⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week08.8.4.p6","kind":"num","q":"Light travels in glass at ⟪2.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫. Calculate the refractive index of the glass. ⟪(c=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1})⟫","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪n=c/v=3.0\\times 10^{8}/2.0\\times 10^{8}⟫ (1) ⟪=1.5⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪n=c/v=3.0\\times 10^{8}/2.0\\times 10^{8}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=1.5⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":1.5,"sf":2,"unit":"","unitTex":""},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Refractive index from angles). Start like this: Formula: ⟪n=\\sin i/\\sin r⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week08.8.4.p7","kind":"num","q":"A ray strikes the surface of water ⟪(n=1.33)⟫ at an angle of incidence of 30°. Calculate the angle of refraction.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪\\sin r=\\sin 30^{\\circ}/1.33⟫ (1) ⟪=0.5/1.33=0.376⟫ (1); ⟪r=22^{\\circ}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪\\sin r=\\sin 30^{\\circ}/1.33⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=0.5/1.33=0.376⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪r=22^{\\circ}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":22,"sf":2,"unit":"°","unitTex":"^{\\circ}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Refractive index from angles). Start like this: Formula: ⟪n=\\sin i/\\sin r⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week08.8.4.p8","kind":"num","q":"Diamond has a refractive index of 2.42. Calculate the speed of light in diamond.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪v=c/n=3.0\\times 10^{8}/2.42⟫ (1) ⟪=1.24\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪v=c/n=3.0\\times 10^{8}/2.42⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=1.24\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":124000000,"sf":3,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Speed of light in a medium). Start like this: Formula: ⟪n=c/v\\rightarrow v=c/n⟫","from":3}}],"exit":[{"id":"week08.8.4.p9","kind":"self","q":"Explain why a glass prism splits white light into a spectrum. State which colour is deviated most.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"White light is a mixture of colours (1); each colour travels at a different speed in glass so is refracted by a different amount / glass has different n for each colour (1); violet is deviated most (1).","points":[{"text":"White light is a mixture of colours","marks":1},{"text":"each colour travels at a different speed in glass so is refracted by a different amount / glass has different n for each colour","marks":1},{"text":"violet is deviated most","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week08.8.4.p10","kind":"self","q":"A student finds the gradient of her sin i against sin r graph to be 1.48. State what this gradient represents and calculate the speed of light in the glass.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Gradient = refractive index of glass (1); ⟪v=c/n=3.0\\times 10^{8}/1.48⟫ (1) ⟪=2.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}\\ (2.03\\times 10^{8})⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Gradient = refractive index of glass","marks":1},{"text":"⟪v=c/n=3.0\\times 10^{8}/1.48⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=2.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}\\ (2.03\\times 10^{8})⟫","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week08.8.4.w1","kind":"self","q":"Define the **normal** to a surface.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"A line at right angles (90°) to the surface at the point of incidence (1).","points":[{"text":"A line at right angles (90°) to the surface at the point of incidence","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week08.8.3.p1"},{"id":"week08.8.4.w2","kind":"self","q":"A ray hits a mirror with an angle of incidence of 35°. What is the angle between the incident ray and the reflected ray?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"⟪35^{\\circ}+35^{\\circ}=70^{\\circ}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪35^{\\circ}+35^{\\circ}=70^{\\circ}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week08.8.3.p3"}],"n":58},{"id":"week08.8.5","kind":"unit","unit":"8.5","title":"Critical Angle and Total Internal Reflection","titleText":"Critical Angle and Total Internal Reflection","goals":[{"code":"C4.11","text":"Explain the critical angle and total internal reflection"},{"code":"C4.12","text":"Apply the relationship ⟪\\sin c=1/n⟫"},{"code":"C4.13","text":"Describe applications of total internal reflection (optical fibres, prisms in periscopes)"}],"codes":["C4.11","C4.12","C4.13"],"words":[{"term":"Interference","def":"The adding or cancelling of two overlapping waves to give a pattern of strong and weak regions (bright and dark fringes)."},{"term":"Normal","def":"A line at 90° to a surface at the point where a ray strikes it."},{"term":"Refraction","def":"Change in direction of light passing from one medium to another, due to a change in speed."},{"term":"Critical angle (c)","def":"Angle of incidence in the denser medium giving an angle of refraction of 90°."},{"term":"Total internal reflection","def":"Complete reflection of light inside a denser medium when i > c."},{"term":"Optical fibre","def":"Thin glass strand that carries light by repeated total internal reflection."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"When light goes from glass (or water) into air it bends **away** from the normal, so r is bigger than i. If we keep making i bigger, r reaches 90° and the ray runs along the surface. Make i any bigger and the light cannot get out at all — it is all reflected back inside."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week08/critical_angle","alt":"Figure 8.12 — Light inside a semicircular glass block meeting the flat glass–air surface","caption":"Figure 8.12 — Light inside a semicircular glass block meeting the flat glass–air surface","width":6.2,"w":1400,"h":400},{"type":"def","term":"Critical angle (c)","text":"the angle of incidence in the denser medium for which the angle of refraction in the less dense medium is 90°."},{"type":"def","term":"Total internal reflection (TIR)","text":"the complete reflection of light back into a denser medium when the angle of incidence is greater than the critical angle."},{"type":"h","text":"Two conditions for TIR"},{"type":"steps","items":["Light must be travelling from a **more dense** (optically denser) medium towards a **less dense** medium (e.g. glass → air, water → air).","The angle of incidence must be **greater than the critical angle** (i > c)."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Angle of incidence (inside glass)","What happens"],"rows":[["i < c","Most light refracts out (bends away from normal); a little is weakly reflected"],["⟪i=c⟫","Refracted ray travels along the surface ⟪(r=90^{\\circ})⟫; strong reflection"],["i > c","**Total internal reflection** — no light leaves; obeys ⟪i=r⟫"]]},{"type":"formula","tex":"\\sin c=1/n","where":["⟪c⟫ = critical angle (°)","⟪n⟫ = refractive index of the denser medium (relative to air)","Comes from Snell's law: ⟪n=\\sin 90^{\\circ}/\\sin c=1/\\sin c⟫"],"label":"CRITICAL ANGLE"},{"type":"table","headers":["Material","n","Critical angle c"],"rows":[["Water","1.33","⟪48.8^{\\circ}\\ (\\approx 49^{\\circ})⟫"],["Glass","1.50","⟪41.8^{\\circ}\\ (\\approx 42^{\\circ})⟫"],["Diamond","2.42","24.4°"]]},{"type":"tip","text":"Higher n → smaller critical angle → TIR happens more easily. Diamond's small c (24°) makes light bounce around inside many times, which is why diamonds sparkle."},{"type":"h","text":"Applications of total internal reflection"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week08/tir_uses","alt":"Figure 8.13 — Optical fibre (left) and prism periscope (right)","caption":"Figure 8.13 — Optical fibre (left) and prism periscope (right)","width":6.2,"w":1400,"h":608},{"type":"p","text":"**1. Optical fibres** — a very thin, flexible strand of glass (core) surrounded by glass of lower refractive index (cladding). Light entering one end hits the core wall at more than the critical angle and is totally internally reflected again and again, so it travels along the fibre even when it bends, with very little loss."},{"type":"bullets","items":["**Telecommunications:** telephone, internet and cable TV signals are sent as pulses of light (much more data, less loss, no electrical interference compared with copper wire).","**Medicine — the endoscope:** a bundle of fibres is put inside the body (e.g. down the throat) — some carry light in, others carry the image back — so doctors can see inside without major surgery.","Decorative lamps and lighting."]},{"type":"p","text":"**2. Prism periscopes and binoculars** — a 45°–45°–90° glass prism can turn light through 90° (periscope) or 180° (binoculars, bicycle reflectors). Light hits the long face inside the glass at 45°, which is greater than the critical angle of glass (42°), so it is totally internally reflected."},{"type":"table","headers":["Why prisms are better than mirrors"],"rows":[["Almost 100% of the light is reflected, so the image is brighter."],["No multiple (ghost) images — a glass mirror reflects from both its front surface and its silvered back."],["No silvering to tarnish or peel off."]]},{"type":"p","text":"**3. Others:** the sparkle of diamonds; cat's eyes road studs and bicycle reflectors; mirages (TIR in hot air near a road)."},{"type":"warn","text":"TIR can NEVER happen when light goes from air into glass or water (less dense → more dense). In that direction the light always bends towards the normal and gets in."}],"examples":[{"id":"week08.8.5.e1","title":"Critical angle of glass","question":"Calculate the critical angle for glass of refractive index 1.50.","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪\\sin c=1/n⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪\\sin c=1/1.50=0.667⟫"},{"label":"Inverse sine","text":"⟪c=\\sin ^{-1}(0.667)=41.8^{\\circ}⟫"}],"answer":"⟪c=41.8^{\\circ}\\ (\\approx 42^{\\circ})⟫"},{"id":"week08.8.5.e2","title":"Refractive index from critical angle","question":"The critical angle of a plastic is 39°. Calculate its refractive index.","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪\\sin c=1/n\\rightarrow n=1/\\sin c⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪n=1/\\sin 39^{\\circ}=1/0.629⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪n=1.59⟫"}],"answer":"⟪n=1.59⟫ (no unit)"},{"id":"week08.8.5.e3","title":"Will TIR happen?","question":"Light inside water ⟪(n=1.33)⟫ strikes the water–air surface at an angle of incidence of 45°. Does it escape or is it totally internally reflected?","steps":[{"label":"Critical angle","text":"⟪\\sin c=1/1.33=0.752\\rightarrow c=48.8^{\\circ}⟫"},{"label":"Compare","text":"⟪i=45^{\\circ}⟫ is LESS than ⟪c=48.8^{\\circ}⟫"},{"label":"Find r (check)","text":"⟪\\sin r=1.33\\times \\sin 45^{\\circ}=1.33\\times 0.707=0.940\\rightarrow r=70^{\\circ}⟫"}],"answer":"i < c, so the light refracts OUT into the air at about 70° to the normal; no TIR."}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week08.8.5.p1","kind":"self","q":"Define **critical angle**.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"The angle of incidence in the denser medium (1) for which the angle of refraction is 90° (1).","points":[{"text":"The angle of incidence in the denser medium","marks":1},{"text":"for which the angle of refraction is 90°","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Critical angle of glass). Start like this: Formula: ⟪\\sin c=1/n⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week08.8.5.p2","kind":"self","q":"State the TWO conditions needed for total internal reflection.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Light travels from a denser to a less dense medium (1); angle of incidence greater than the critical angle (1).","points":[{"text":"Light travels from a denser to a less dense medium","marks":1},{"text":"angle of incidence greater than the critical angle","marks":1}],"section":"try"}],"practice":[{"id":"week08.8.5.p3","kind":"num","q":"Calculate the critical angle for water, ⟪n=1.33⟫.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪\\sin c=1/1.33=0.752⟫ (1); ⟪c=48.8^{\\circ}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪\\sin c=1/1.33=0.752⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪c=48.8^{\\circ}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":48.8,"sf":3,"unit":"°","unitTex":"^{\\circ}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Critical angle of glass). Start like this: Formula: ⟪\\sin c=1/n⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week08.8.5.p4","kind":"num","q":"The critical angle of a type of glass is 38.7°. Calculate its refractive index.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪n=1/\\sin 38.7^{\\circ}=1/0.625⟫ (1) ⟪=1.60⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪n=1/\\sin 38.7^{\\circ}=1/0.625⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=1.60⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":1.6,"sf":3,"unit":"","unitTex":""},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Critical angle of glass). Start like this: Formula: ⟪\\sin c=1/n⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week08.8.5.p5","kind":"self","q":"A ray inside a glass block ⟪(n=1.50)⟫ meets the glass–air surface at 50°. State what happens to the ray and explain why.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Critical angle ⟪=41.8^{\\circ}⟫ (1); 50° > 41.8° (1); so the ray is totally internally reflected (at 50° to the normal) (1).","points":[{"text":"Critical angle ⟪=41.8^{\\circ}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"50° > 41.8°","marks":1},{"text":"so the ray is totally internally reflected (at 50° to the normal)","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week08.8.5.p6","kind":"self","q":"Explain how light travels along a curved optical fibre.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Light enters the fibre / core (1); it hits the inside wall at an angle greater than the critical angle (1); it is totally internally reflected repeatedly along the fibre (1).","points":[{"text":"Light enters the fibre / core","marks":1},{"text":"it hits the inside wall at an angle greater than the critical angle","marks":1},{"text":"it is totally internally reflected repeatedly along the fibre","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week08.8.5.p7","kind":"self","q":"State TWO uses of optical fibres.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Any two: telecommunications/internet/telephone (1); endoscope in medicine (1); decorative lighting (1).","points":[{"text":"Any two: telecommunications/internet/telephone","marks":1},{"text":"endoscope in medicine","marks":1},{"text":"decorative lighting","marks":1}],"conf":"low","section":"practice"}],"exit":[{"id":"week08.8.5.p8","kind":"self","q":"Give TWO reasons why prisms are used instead of plane mirrors in good periscopes.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Any two: nearly all light reflected so brighter image (1); no multiple images (1); no silvering to tarnish (1).","points":[{"text":"Any two: nearly all light reflected so brighter image","marks":1},{"text":"no multiple images","marks":1},{"text":"no silvering to tarnish","marks":1}],"conf":"low","section":"exit"},{"id":"week08.8.5.p9","kind":"self","q":"Explain why a 45° prism made of glass with ⟪n=1.50⟫ can be used in a periscope, but one made of a material with ⟪n=1.30⟫ cannot.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":5,"scheme":"For ⟪n=1.50⟫, ⟪c=\\sin ^{-1}(1/1.50)=41.8^{\\circ}⟫ (1); 45° > 41.8° so TIR occurs (1). For ⟪n=1.30⟫, ⟪c=\\sin ^{-1}(1/1.30)=50.3^{\\circ}⟫ (1); 45° < 50.3° so light refracts out, no TIR (1).","points":[{"text":"For ⟪n=1.50⟫, ⟪c=\\sin ^{-1}(1/1.50)=41.8^{\\circ}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"45° > 41.8° so TIR occurs","marks":1},{"text":"For ⟪n=1.30⟫, ⟪c=\\sin ^{-1}(1/1.30)=50.3^{\\circ}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"45° < 50.3° so light refracts out, no TIR","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week08.8.5.w1","kind":"num","q":"Light enters a liquid from air. ⟪i=50^{\\circ}⟫, ⟪r=32^{\\circ}⟫. Calculate the refractive index of the liquid.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪n=\\sin 50^{\\circ}/\\sin 32^{\\circ}=0.766/0.530⟫ (1) ⟪=1.45⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪n=\\sin 50^{\\circ}/\\sin 32^{\\circ}=0.766/0.530⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=1.45⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":1.45,"sf":3,"unit":"","unitTex":""},"section":"warmup","src":"week08.8.4.p5"},{"id":"week08.8.5.w2","kind":"self","q":"Give TWO everyday observations that are evidence of refraction.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Any two: pool looks shallower than it is (1); stick looks bent in water (1); coin in cup appears when water is added (1).","points":[{"text":"Any two: pool looks shallower than it is","marks":1},{"text":"stick looks bent in water","marks":1},{"text":"coin in cup appears when water is added","marks":1}],"conf":"low","section":"warmup","src":"week08.8.4.p2"},{"id":"week08.8.5.w3","kind":"self","q":"A ray of light travels along the normal into a glass block. What happens to its direction and its speed?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Direction unchanged / not bent (1); speed decreases (1).","points":[{"text":"Direction unchanged / not bent","marks":1},{"text":"speed decreases","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week08.8.4.p4"}],"n":59},{"id":"week08.8.6","kind":"unit","unit":"8.6","title":"Lenses: Focal Length, Ray Diagrams, Magnification and the Lens Formula","titleText":"Lenses: Focal Length, Ray Diagrams, Magnification and the Lens Formula","goals":[{"code":"C5.1","text":"Describe the effect of converging and diverging lenses on a parallel beam of light"},{"code":"C5.2","text":"Define principal axis, principal focus, focal length, focal plane and magnification"},{"code":"C5.3","text":"Distinguish between real and virtual images and draw ray diagrams to locate images formed by a converging lens"},{"code":"C5.4","text":"Apply magnification = image height / object height = image distance / object distance (v/u)"},{"code":"C5.5","text":"Determine the focal length of a converging lens (distant object method; ⟪1/f=1/u+1/v⟫)"}],"codes":["C5.1","C5.2","C5.3","C5.4","C5.5"],"words":[{"term":"Virtual image","def":"An image from which rays only appear to come; cannot be formed on a screen."},{"term":"Real image","def":"An image formed where rays actually meet; can be formed on a screen."},{"term":"Refraction","def":"Change in direction of light passing from one medium to another, due to a change in speed."},{"term":"Focal length (f)","def":"Distance from the optical centre to the principal focus."},{"term":"Magnification","def":"Image height ÷ object height ⟪(=v/u)⟫."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"h","text":"Two types of lens"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week08/lens_parallel","alt":"Figure 8.14 — Effect of a converging lens (left) and a diverging lens (right) on a parallel beam","caption":"Figure 8.14 — Effect of a converging lens (left) and a diverging lens (right) on a parallel beam","width":6,"w":1400,"h":554},{"type":"table","headers":["","Converging (convex) lens","Diverging (concave) lens"],"rows":[["Shape","thicker in the middle","thinner in the middle"],["Symbol in diagrams","vertical line with arrowheads pointing OUT at both ends","vertical line with arrowheads pointing IN at both ends"],["Effect on parallel rays","**converges** them to a real focus F on the other side","**diverges** them so they appear to come from a virtual focus F on the same side"],["Uses","magnifying glass, camera, projector, eye, spectacles for long sight","spectacles for short sight, door peephole"]]},{"type":"h","text":"Definitions"},{"type":"def","term":"Principal axis","text":"the line passing through the optical centre of the lens at right angles to the lens."},{"type":"def","term":"Optical centre (C)","text":"the centre of the lens; a ray through it passes straight through without bending."},{"type":"def","term":"Principal focus (F) of a converging lens","text":"the point on the principal axis to which rays parallel and close to the axis converge after refraction by the lens."},{"type":"def","term":"Principal focus (F) of a diverging lens","text":"the point on the principal axis from which rays parallel and close to the axis appear to diverge after refraction by the lens."},{"type":"def","term":"Focal length (f)","text":"the distance between the optical centre and the principal focus."},{"type":"def","term":"Focal plane","text":"the plane through the principal focus, at right angles to the principal axis. Parallel rays NOT parallel to the axis are focused at a point on this plane."},{"type":"def","term":"Magnification (m)","text":"the ratio of image height to object height."},{"type":"remember","text":"A lens has a principal focus on EACH side, both at the same distance f from the lens. A thicker (more curved) lens has a shorter focal length — it is more powerful."},{"type":"h","text":"Real and virtual images"},{"type":"table","headers":["Real image","Virtual image"],"rows":[["Formed where rays ACTUALLY meet","Rays only APPEAR to come from it"],["Can be caught on a screen","Cannot be caught on a screen"],["Always inverted (for a single lens)","Upright"],["On the opposite side of the lens to the object","On the same side as the object"],["Drawn with solid lines","Drawn with dashed lines"]]},{"type":"h","text":"Drawing ray diagrams for a converging lens"},{"type":"p","text":"You only need TWO of these three rays from the top of the object. Where they meet is the top of the image."},{"type":"steps","items":["A ray **parallel to the principal axis** is refracted **through F** on the other side.","A ray through the **optical centre** goes **straight on** without bending.","A ray through F on the object side comes out **parallel** to the principal axis."]},{"type":"img","src":"img/week08/lens_cases","alt":"Figure 8.15 — Images formed by a converging lens for three object positions","caption":"Figure 8.15 — Images formed by a converging lens for three object positions","width":5.6,"w":1183,"h":1603},{"type":"table","headers":["Object position","Image position","Nature of image","Use"],"rows":[["Very far away (at infinity)","at F","real, inverted, very diminished (a point)","finding f quickly"],["Beyond 2F","between F and 2F","real, inverted, diminished","camera, eye"],["At 2F","at 2F","real, inverted, same size","photocopier (same size copy)"],["Between F and 2F","beyond 2F","real, inverted, magnified","projector, slide viewer"],["At F","at infinity (rays parallel)","no image formed","spotlight/searchlight"],["Between F and lens","same side as object","**virtual, upright, magnified**","magnifying glass"]]},{"type":"tip","text":"Ray diagrams in exams: use a sharp pencil and ruler; put arrows on rays; draw the lens as a straight vertical line with the correct arrowheads; mark F on BOTH sides; dashed lines for virtual rays and virtual images. Usually 1 mark per correct ray, 1 for the image, 1 for the arrows."},{"type":"h","text":"Magnification and the lens formula"},{"type":"formula","tex":"\\text{magnification }m=\\text{ image height }/\\text{ object height }=v/u","where":["⟪v⟫ = image distance (lens to image)","⟪u⟫ = object distance (lens to object)","m has no unit; m > 1 means magnified, m < 1 means diminished"],"label":"MAGNIFICATION"},{"type":"formula","tex":"1/f=1/u+1/v","where":["⟪f⟫ = focal length, u = object distance, v = image distance (all in the same unit, e.g. cm)","Sign rule (\"real is positive\"): distances to REAL images and objects are +; a VIRTUAL image has a NEGATIVE v","f is + for a converging lens"],"label":"LENS FORMULA"},{"type":"warn","text":"Most common lens mistake: forgetting the final step. ⟪1/v=0.0333⟫ does NOT mean ⟪v=0.0333⟫. You must take the reciprocal: ⟪v=1\\div 0.0333=30\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫."},{"type":"h","text":"Experiment: determining the focal length of a converging lens"},{"type":"p","text":"**Method 1 — distant object (quick estimate)**"},{"type":"steps","items":["Stand at the back of the room and point the lens at a distant window or tree outside.","Move a white screen (or paper) behind the lens until a **sharp, inverted image** of the distant object is seen.","Measure the distance from the lens to the screen with a metre rule. Rays from a distant object are almost parallel, so this distance ≈ **focal length f**."]},{"type":"p","text":"**Method 2 — using u and v (more accurate)**"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week08/focal_expt","alt":"Figure 8.16 — Apparatus for finding the focal length using u and v","caption":"Figure 8.16 — Apparatus for finding the focal length using u and v","width":5.2,"w":1048,"h":534},{"type":"steps","items":["Set up an illuminated object (lamp box with cross-wire), the lens in a holder and a screen in a straight line on a metre rule (the centres at the same height).","Place the lens a distance u from the object (u greater than f — start at about 2–3 × your estimate).","Move the screen until a **sharp** image is formed. Measure v (lens to screen).","Repeat for at least 5 different values of u.","Calculate f from ⟪1/f=1/u+1/v⟫ for each pair, and average. **OR** plot 1/v (y-axis) against 1/u (x-axis): a straight line with gradient −1; **each intercept **⟪\\boldsymbol{=1/f}⟫."]},{"type":"p","text":"**Precautions:** find the sharpest image by moving the screen back and forth; keep the object, lens centre and screen in a straight line at the same height; read the rule at eye level (avoid parallax); work in a darkened room so the image is easy to see; repeat readings."}],"examples":[{"id":"week08.8.6.e1","title":"Ray diagram to scale","question":"An object 5.0 cm tall is placed 15 cm from a converging lens of focal length 10 cm. By drawing a ray diagram to scale, find the position, size and nature of the image. (Scale: 1 cm on paper ⟪=5\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫.)","img":{"src":"img/week08/we_lens","alt":"An object 5.0 cm tall is placed 15 cm from a converging lens of focal length 10 cm. By drawing a ray diagram to scale, find the position, size and nature of the image. (Scale: 1 cm on paper = 5 cm.)","w":1151,"h":574},"steps":[{"label":"Draw","text":"Principal axis; lens at centre; F at ⟪2\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\ (=10\\,\\mathrm{cm})⟫ each side; object ⟪3\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\ (=15\\,\\mathrm{cm})⟫ from lens, ⟪1\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\ (=5\\,\\mathrm{cm})⟫ tall."},{"label":"Ray 1","text":"From top of object, parallel to axis, then through F on the far side."},{"label":"Ray 2","text":"From top of object straight through the optical centre."},{"label":"Read off","text":"Rays meet ⟪6\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\ (=30\\,\\mathrm{cm})⟫ from the lens, ⟪2\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\ (=10\\,\\mathrm{cm})⟫ below the axis."},{"label":"Check with formula","text":"⟪1/v=1/10-1/15=0.1000-0.0667=0.0333\\rightarrow v=30\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫; ⟪m=v/u=30/15=2⟫"}],"answer":"Image 30 cm from the lens on the other side, 10 cm tall: real, inverted, magnified ⟪(\\times 2)⟫."},{"id":"week08.8.6.e2","title":"Using the lens formula","question":"An object is placed 30 cm from a converging lens of focal length 10 cm. Calculate (a) the image distance, (b) the magnification.","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪1/f=1/u+1/v\\rightarrow 1/v=1/f-1/u⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪1/v=1/10-1/30=0.1000-0.0333=0.0667⟫"},{"label":"Reciprocal","text":"⟪v=1\\div 0.0667=15\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫"},{"label":"Magnification","text":"⟪m=v/u=15/30=0.50⟫"}],"answer":"(a) ⟪v=15\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫ (real image)  (b) ⟪m=0.50⟫ (diminished)"},{"id":"week08.8.6.e3","title":"A magnifying glass (virtual image)","question":"A stamp is held 10 cm from a converging lens of focal length 20 cm. Find the image distance and magnification.","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪1/v=1/f-1/u⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪1/v=1/20-1/10=0.05-0.10=-0.05⟫"},{"label":"Reciprocal","text":"⟪v=1\\div (-0.05)=-20\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫"},{"label":"Meaning of minus","text":"Negative v → VIRTUAL image, on the same side as the stamp"},{"label":"Magnification","text":"⟪m=v/u=20/10=2⟫ (use the size of v)"}],"answer":"Image is virtual, upright, 20 cm from the lens on the same side as the object; magnification ⟪=2⟫."},{"id":"week08.8.6.e4","title":"Finding f from an experiment","question":"In an experiment a sharp image was obtained on a screen 60 cm from a lens when the object was 30 cm from the lens. Calculate the focal length.","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪1/f=1/u+1/v⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪1/f=1/30+1/60=0.0333+0.0167=0.0500⟫"},{"label":"Reciprocal","text":"⟪f=1\\div 0.0500=20\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫"}],"answer":"⟪f=20\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫"}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week08.8.6.p1","kind":"self","q":"Define the **principal focus** of a converging lens.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"The point on the principal axis (1) to which rays parallel (and close) to the principal axis converge after passing through the lens (1).","points":[{"text":"The point on the principal axis","marks":1},{"text":"to which rays parallel (and close) to the principal axis converge after passing through the lens","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Ray diagram to scale). Start like this: Draw: Principal axis; lens at centre; F at ⟪2\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\ (=10\\,\\mathrm{cm})⟫ each side; object ⟪3\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\ (=15\\,\\mathrm{cm})⟫ from lens, ⟪1\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\ (=5\\,\\mathrm{cm})⟫ tall.","from":1}},{"id":"week08.8.6.p2","kind":"self","q":"Define **focal length**.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"Distance between the optical centre and the principal focus (1).","points":[{"text":"Distance between the optical centre and the principal focus","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Ray diagram to scale). Start like this: Draw: Principal axis; lens at centre; F at ⟪2\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\ (=10\\,\\mathrm{cm})⟫ each side; object ⟪3\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\ (=15\\,\\mathrm{cm})⟫ from lens, ⟪1\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\ (=5\\,\\mathrm{cm})⟫ tall.","from":1}}],"practice":[{"id":"week08.8.6.p3","kind":"self","q":"Draw a diagram to show the effect of a diverging lens on a beam of light parallel to the principal axis. Mark F.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":5,"scheme":"Correct diverging lens symbol (1); rays spread out after the lens (1); dashed extensions back to meet at F on the incident side (1).","points":[{"text":"Correct diverging lens symbol","marks":1},{"text":"rays spread out after the lens","marks":1},{"text":"dashed extensions back to meet at F on the incident side","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week08.8.6.p4","kind":"self","q":"State TWO differences between a real image and a virtual image.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Real: rays actually meet / virtual: rays only appear to come from it (1). Real can be formed on a screen; virtual cannot (1). (Also accept: real inverted, virtual upright.)","points":[{"text":"Real: rays actually meet / virtual: rays only appear to come from it","marks":1},{"text":"Real can be formed on a screen; virtual cannot (Also accept: real inverted, virtual upright.)","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (A magnifying glass (virtual image)). Start like this: Formula: ⟪1/v=1/f-1/u⟫","from":3}},{"id":"week08.8.6.p5","kind":"num","q":"An object 2.0 cm tall forms an image 6.0 cm tall. Calculate the magnification.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"⟪m=6.0/2.0=3.0⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪m=6.0/2.0=3.0⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":3,"sf":2,"unit":"","unitTex":""},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Using the lens formula). Start like this: Formula: ⟪1/f=1/u+1/v\\rightarrow 1/v=1/f-1/u⟫","from":2}},{"id":"week08.8.6.p6","kind":"self","q":"An object is placed 20 cm from a converging lens of focal length 10 cm. (a) Calculate the image distance. (b) Describe the image.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"⟪1/v=1/10-1/20=0.05⟫ (1); ⟪v=20\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫ (1); real, inverted (1), same size ⟪(m=1)⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪1/v=1/10-1/20=0.05⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪v=20\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"real, inverted","marks":1},{"text":"same size ⟪(m=1)⟫","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Using the lens formula). Start like this: Formula: ⟪1/f=1/u+1/v\\rightarrow 1/v=1/f-1/u⟫","from":2}},{"id":"week08.8.6.p7","kind":"self","q":"An object is placed 36 cm from a converging lens of focal length 12 cm. Calculate (a) v and (b) the magnification.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"⟪1/v=1/12-1/36=0.0556⟫ (1); ⟪v=18\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫ (1); ⟪m=v/u=18/36⟫ (1) ⟪=0.50⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪1/v=1/12-1/36=0.0556⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪v=18\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪m=v/u=18/36⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=0.50⟫","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Using the lens formula). Start like this: Formula: ⟪1/f=1/u+1/v\\rightarrow 1/v=1/f-1/u⟫","from":2}},{"id":"week08.8.6.p8","kind":"self","q":"The diagram shows an object O in front of a converging lens. Each division represents 2 cm. Complete the ray diagram to locate the image, and state its nature.","marks":4,"level":3,"img":{"src":"img/week08/lens_practice","alt":"The diagram shows an object O in front of a converging lens. Each division represents 2 cm. Complete the ray diagram to locate the image, and state its nature.","w":1214,"h":431},"lines":3,"scheme":"F is 4 cm from the lens and O is 10 cm away (beyond 2F). Ray parallel to axis then through F (1); ray straight through optical centre (1); image located between F and 2F on the far side, about 6.7 cm (≈ 3.3 divisions) from the lens (1); real, inverted, diminished (1).","points":[{"text":"F is 4 cm from the lens and O is 10 cm away (beyond 2F). Ray parallel to axis then through F","marks":1},{"text":"ray straight through optical centre","marks":1},{"text":"image located between F and 2F on the far side, about 6.7 cm (≈ 3.3 divisions) from the lens","marks":1},{"text":"real, inverted, diminished","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Ray diagram to scale). Start like this: Draw: Principal axis; lens at centre; F at ⟪2\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\ (=10\\,\\mathrm{cm})⟫ each side; object ⟪3\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\ (=15\\,\\mathrm{cm})⟫ from lens, ⟪1\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\ (=5\\,\\mathrm{cm})⟫ tall.","from":1}}],"exit":[{"id":"week08.8.6.p9","kind":"self","q":"A student holds a lens of focal length 15 cm at 10 cm from a page of print. Calculate the image distance and magnification, and state whether the image is real or virtual.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"⟪1/v=1/15-1/10=-0.0333⟫ (1); ⟪v=-30\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫ (1); virtual (on same side as the page) (1); ⟪m=30/10=3⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪1/v=1/15-1/10=-0.0333⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪v=-30\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"virtual (on same side as the page)","marks":1},{"text":"⟪m=30/10=3⟫","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week08.8.6.p10","kind":"self","q":"Describe how you would quickly find the approximate focal length of a converging lens using a distant window.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Point the lens at a distant object/window and hold a screen behind it (1); move the screen until a sharp (inverted) image is formed (1); measure the distance from lens to screen with a metre rule — this equals f (1).","points":[{"text":"Point the lens at a distant object/window and hold a screen behind it","marks":1},{"text":"move the screen until a sharp (inverted) image is formed","marks":1},{"text":"measure the distance from lens to screen with a metre rule — this equals f","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week08.8.6.w1","kind":"self","q":"Define **critical angle**.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"The angle of incidence in the denser medium (1) for which the angle of refraction is 90° (1).","points":[{"text":"The angle of incidence in the denser medium","marks":1},{"text":"for which the angle of refraction is 90°","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week08.8.5.p1"},{"id":"week08.8.6.w2","kind":"num","q":"The critical angle of a type of glass is 38.7°. Calculate its refractive index.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪n=1/\\sin 38.7^{\\circ}=1/0.625⟫ (1) ⟪=1.60⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪n=1/\\sin 38.7^{\\circ}=1/0.625⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=1.60⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":1.6,"sf":3,"unit":"","unitTex":""},"section":"warmup","src":"week08.8.5.p4"},{"id":"week08.8.6.w3","kind":"self","q":"State the TWO conditions needed for total internal reflection.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Light travels from a denser to a less dense medium (1); angle of incidence greater than the critical angle (1).","points":[{"text":"Light travels from a denser to a less dense medium","marks":1},{"text":"angle of incidence greater than the critical angle","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week08.8.5.p2"}],"n":60},{"id":"week08.check","kind":"check","title":"Book check","titleText":"Book check","check":true,"watchOut":["Measuring angles from the SURFACE instead of from the NORMAL. Always draw the normal first; if given the angle to the surface, subtract from 90°.","Writing ⟪n=i/r⟫ (dividing the angles). It is ⟪n=\\sin i/\\sin r⟫.","Calculator in radians mode — check ⟪\\sin 30=0.5⟫ before the exam starts.","Forgetting to take the reciprocal at the end of the lens formula (writing ⟪v=0.0333⟫ instead of ⟪v=30\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫).","Saying TIR happens when light goes from air into glass. TIR only happens from a denser to a less dense medium, and only when i > c.","Drawing virtual rays and virtual images with solid lines, or drawing the lens symbol wrongly (converging: arrowheads out; diverging: arrowheads in).","Stating only one law of reflection (forgetting \"same plane\").","Thinking the frequency or colour of light changes on refraction — only speed and wavelength change.","Calling a lunar eclipse a solar eclipse: in a SOLAR eclipse the Moon is in the middle; in a LUNAR eclipse the Earth is in the middle.","Mixing units in the lens formula (u in m, f in cm). Put all distances in the same unit."],"mcq":[{"id":"week08.check.m1","kind":"mcq","q":"Which scientist proposed that light is made up of tiny particles called corpuscles?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["Huygens","Young","Newton","Foucault"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Newton proposed the corpuscular theory. Huygens (A) proposed the wave theory; Young's experiment supported waves.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week08.check.m2","kind":"mcq","q":"Young's double slit experiment provided evidence that light","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["travels in straight lines","is a wave","is made of particles","travels at ⟪3\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Interference fringes can only be produced by waves. Straight-line travel (A) does not distinguish the theories.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week08.check.m3","kind":"mcq","q":"Diffraction of light is not usually noticed because","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["light travels too fast","the wavelength of light is very small compared with everyday gaps","light is a transverse wave","light does not need a medium"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Diffraction is noticeable only when the gap ≈ wavelength. Speed (A) has nothing to do with the amount of diffraction.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week08.check.m4","kind":"mcq","q":"During a solar eclipse","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["the Earth is between the Sun and the Moon","the Moon is between the Sun and the Earth","the Sun is between the Earth and the Moon","the Moon is in the Earth's shadow"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The Moon blocks the Sun, casting its shadow on Earth. A and D describe a LUNAR eclipse.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week08.check.m5","kind":"mcq","q":"The image in a pinhole camera is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["virtual and upright","real and upright","virtual and inverted","real and inverted"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Rays cross at the pinhole, so the image is inverted, and it forms on a screen, so it is real.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week08.check.m6","kind":"mcq","q":"A ray of light strikes a plane mirror at an angle of 20° to the surface. The angle of reflection is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["20°","40°","70°","140°"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Angles are measured from the normal: ⟪i=90^{\\circ}-20^{\\circ}=70^{\\circ}⟫, so ⟪r=70^{\\circ}⟫. 20° (A) is the angle to the surface, not the normal.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week08.check.m7","kind":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is NOT a property of the image formed in a plane mirror?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["virtual","same size as the object","laterally inverted","real"],"answer":"D","explanation":"A plane mirror image is virtual — it cannot be caught on a screen. A, B and C are all true properties.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week08.check.m8","kind":"mcq","q":"When light passes from air into glass, which quantity remains unchanged?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["speed","wavelength","frequency","direction (for i > 0°)"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Frequency is set by the source and does not change. Speed and wavelength both decrease; direction changes for i > 0°.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week08.check.m9","kind":"mcq","q":"A ray of light enters a glass block at an angle of incidence of 45°. The angle of refraction is 28°. The refractive index of the glass is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["0.66","1.51","1.61","17"],"answer":"B","explanation":"⟪n=\\sin 45^{\\circ}/\\sin 28^{\\circ}=0.707/0.469=1.51⟫. 1.61 (C) is ⟪45\\div 28⟫ — the angles themselves must not be divided.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week08.check.m10","kind":"mcq","q":"Light travels at ⟪2.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ in a liquid. The refractive index of the liquid is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["0.67","1.5","2.0","6.0"],"answer":"B","explanation":"⟪n=c/v=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\div 2.0\\times 10^{8}=1.5⟫. 0.67 (A) is v/c, upside down — n is always greater than 1.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week08.check.m11","kind":"mcq","q":"When white light passes through a prism, the colour deviated most is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["red","yellow","green","violet"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Violet is slowed the most in glass, so it is refracted the most. Red (A) is deviated least.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week08.check.m12","kind":"mcq","q":"The critical angle of a glass is 42°. Total internal reflection will occur when light travels from","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["air to glass at 50°","glass to air at 30°","glass to air at 50°","air to glass at 30°"],"answer":"C","explanation":"TIR needs denser → less dense AND i > c: glass → air at 50° > 42°. B is glass → air but 30° < 42°, so the light refracts out.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week08.check.m13","kind":"mcq","q":"The refractive index of a material is 2.0. Its critical angle is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["30°","45°","60°","90°"],"answer":"A","explanation":"⟪\\sin c=1/n=1/2.0=0.5⟫, so ⟪c=30^{\\circ}⟫. 60° (C) is the angle whose COSINE is 0.5.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week08.check.m14","kind":"mcq","q":"An object is placed between F and the optical centre of a converging lens. The image is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["real, inverted and magnified","virtual, upright and magnified","real, inverted and diminished","virtual, upright and diminished"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Object inside F → magnifying glass: virtual, upright, magnified. A is for an object between F and 2F.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week08.check.m15","kind":"mcq","q":"An object 20 cm from a converging lens gives a sharp image on a screen 20 cm on the other side. The focal length of the lens is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["5 cm","10 cm","20 cm","40 cm"],"answer":"B","explanation":"⟪1/f=1/20+1/20=2/20\\rightarrow f=10\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫ (object at 2F gives image at 2F). 40 cm (D) comes from adding ⟪u+v⟫.","section":"p01"}],"structured":[{"id":"week08.check.s1","kind":"multi","title":"Refraction and total internal reflection","total":13,"stem":["A ray of light in air strikes the surface of a semicircular glass block, of refractive index 1.50, and travels towards the centre of the flat face."],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week08.check.s1.a","kind":"self","q":"State what is meant by the term **refractive index** in terms of the speed of light.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Ratio of the speed of light in vacuum/air (1) to the speed of light in the medium ⟪(n=c/v)⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Ratio of the speed of light in vacuum/air","marks":1},{"text":"to the speed of light in the medium ⟪(n=c/v)⟫","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week08.check.s1.b","kind":"self","q":"Calculate the speed of light in the glass.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪v=c/n=3.0\\times 10^{8}/1.50⟫ (1) ⟪=2.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪v=c/n=3.0\\times 10^{8}/1.50⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=2.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":200000000,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"label":"(b)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week08.check.s1.c","kind":"self","q":"Define **critical angle** and calculate its value for this glass.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"Angle of incidence in the denser medium (1) for which angle of refraction is 90° (1); ⟪\\sin c=1/1.50=0.667⟫ (1); ⟪c=41.8^{\\circ}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Angle of incidence in the denser medium","marks":1},{"text":"for which angle of refraction is 90°","marks":1},{"text":"⟪\\sin c=1/1.50=0.667⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪c=41.8^{\\circ}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":41.8,"sf":3,"unit":"°","unitTex":"^{\\circ}"},"label":"(c)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week08.check.s1.d","kind":"self","q":"The ray meets the flat face, inside the glass, at an angle of incidence of 30°. Calculate the angle of refraction as it leaves into the air.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Inside glass to air: ⟪\\sin r=n\\times \\sin i=1.50\\times \\sin 30^{\\circ}⟫ (1) ⟪=0.75⟫ (1); ⟪r=48.6^{\\circ}\\ (49^{\\circ})⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Inside glass to air: ⟪\\sin r=n\\times \\sin i=1.50\\times \\sin 30^{\\circ}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=0.75⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪r=48.6^{\\circ}\\ (49^{\\circ})⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":48.6,"sf":3,"unit":"°","unitTex":"^{\\circ}","alt":[49]},"label":"(d)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week08.check.s1.e","kind":"self","q":"The angle of incidence at the flat face is increased to 60°. Describe what happens to the ray and give a reason.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Total internal reflection occurs / ray reflected back into the glass (1) because 60° is greater than the critical angle 41.8° (1).","points":[{"text":"Total internal reflection occurs / ray reflected back into the glass","marks":1},{"text":"because 60° is greater than the critical angle 41.8°","marks":1}],"label":"(e)"}]},{"id":"week08.check.s2","kind":"multi","title":"The converging lens","total":13,"stem":["A converging lens has a focal length of 8.0 cm. An object 3.0 cm tall is placed 24 cm from the lens."],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week08.check.s2.a","kind":"self","q":"Define the **principal focus** of a converging lens.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Point on the principal axis (1) to which rays parallel to the axis converge after refraction through the lens (1).","points":[{"text":"Point on the principal axis","marks":1},{"text":"to which rays parallel to the axis converge after refraction through the lens","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week08.check.s2.b","kind":"self","q":"Use the lens formula to calculate the image distance.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪1/v=1/f-1/u=1/8.0-1/24⟫ (1) ⟪=0.125-0.0417=0.0833⟫ (1); ⟪v=12\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪1/v=1/f-1/u=1/8.0-1/24⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=0.125-0.0417=0.0833⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪v=12\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":12,"sf":2,"unit":"cm","unitTex":"\\mathrm{cm}"},"label":"(b)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week08.check.s2.c","kind":"self","q":"Calculate the magnification and the height of the image.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪m=v/u=12/24=0.50⟫ (1); image height ⟪=m⟫ × object height (1) ⟪=0.50\\times 3.0=1.5\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪m=v/u=12/24=0.50⟫","marks":1},{"text":"image height ⟪=m⟫ × object height","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=0.50\\times 3.0=1.5\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week08.check.s2.d","kind":"self","q":"State THREE characteristics of the image.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Real (1); inverted (1); diminished (1).","points":[{"text":"Real","marks":1},{"text":"inverted","marks":1},{"text":"diminished","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"},{"id":"week08.check.s2.e","kind":"self","q":"The object is moved to 5.0 cm from the lens. State TWO ways in which the image changes.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Any two: image becomes virtual (1); upright (1); magnified (1); on the same side as the object (1).","points":[{"text":"Any two: image becomes virtual","marks":1},{"text":"upright","marks":1},{"text":"magnified","marks":1},{"text":"on the same side as the object","marks":1}],"conf":"low","label":"(e)"}]},{"id":"week08.check.s3","kind":"multi","title":"The nature of light and reflection","total":11,"stem":["Scientists have argued for centuries about the nature of light."],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week08.check.s3.a","kind":"self","q":"Briefly describe Newton's and Huygens' theories of light.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Newton: light consists of particles/corpuscles (1). Huygens: light is a wave (1).","points":[{"text":"Newton: light consists of particles/corpuscles","marks":1},{"text":"Huygens: light is a wave","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week08.check.s3.b","kind":"self","q":"Describe Young's double slit experiment and explain how its result supports one of these theories.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":6,"scheme":"Monochromatic light through single slit then double slit (1); light diffracts and overlaps (1); bright and dark fringes seen on screen (1); interference — only waves can do this, supporting Huygens' wave theory (1).","points":[{"text":"Monochromatic light through single slit then double slit","marks":1},{"text":"light diffracts and overlaps","marks":1},{"text":"bright and dark fringes seen on screen","marks":1},{"text":"interference — only waves can do this, supporting Huygens' wave theory","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week08.check.s3.c","kind":"self","q":"State the TWO laws of reflection.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Incident ray, reflected ray and normal lie in the same plane (1); angle of incidence = angle of reflection (1).","points":[{"text":"Incident ray, reflected ray and normal lie in the same plane","marks":1},{"text":"angle of incidence = angle of reflection","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week08.check.s3.d","kind":"self","q":"A boy stands 1.5 m in front of a plane mirror. He then walks 0.5 m towards it. By how much does the distance between the boy and his image decrease?","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Initial boy–image distance ⟪=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1); new ⟪=2\\times 1.0=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1); decrease ⟪=1.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Initial boy–image distance ⟪=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"new ⟪=2\\times 1.0=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"decrease ⟪=1.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"}]}],"n":61},{"id":"week08.past","kind":"past","title":"Past-paper practice","titleText":"Past-paper practice","check":true,"log":true,"questions":[{"id":"week08.past.q1","kind":"multi","title":"Data analysis (Paper 02 Question 1 style): Snell's law","total":25,"stem":["A student traced rays of light through a rectangular glass block using optical pins. For each angle of incidence, i, she measured the angle of refraction, r. Her results are shown below."],"stemBlocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["i / °","10","20","30","40","50","60"],"rows":[["r / °","7","13","20","25","31","35"],["sin i","","","","","",""],["sin r","","","","","",""]]}],"parts":[{"id":"week08.past.q1.a","kind":"self","q":"Complete the table by calculating sin i and sin r, each to 2 decimal places.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":0,"scheme":"sin i: 0.17, 0.34, 0.50, 0.64, 0.77, 0.87 (2: −1 per error); sin r: 0.12, 0.22, 0.34, 0.42, 0.52, 0.57 (2: −1 per error).","points":[{"text":"sin i: 0.17, 0.34, 0.50, 0.64, 0.77, 0.87","marks":2},{"text":"sin r: 0.12, 0.22, 0.34, 0.42, 0.52, 0.57","marks":2}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week08.past.q1.b","kind":"self","q":"On the grid provided, plot a graph of sin i (y-axis) against sin r (x-axis). Draw the best straight line.","marks":7,"level":3,"img":{"src":"img/week08/graph_paper","alt":"On the grid provided, plot a graph of sin i (y-axis) against sin r (x-axis). Draw the best straight line.","w":1174,"h":894},"lines":0,"scheme":"Axes labelled sin i and sin r (1); suitable scale using more than half the grid (1); all 6 points correctly plotted (3: −1 per error); best straight line passing through/near the origin (1); line drawn thin and with a ruler (1).","points":[{"text":"Axes labelled sin i and sin r","marks":1},{"text":"suitable scale using more than half the grid","marks":1},{"text":"all 6 points correctly plotted","marks":3},{"text":"best straight line passing through/near the origin","marks":1},{"text":"line drawn thin and with a ruler","marks":1}],"label":"(b)","grid":true},{"id":"week08.past.q1.c","kind":"self","q":"Determine the gradient of your graph. Show clearly the triangle you used.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"Large triangle drawn (at least half the line) (1); correct readings of Δ(sin i) and Δ(sin r) from the line (1); gradient ⟪=\\Delta (\\sin i)/\\Delta (\\sin r)⟫ (1); ≈ 1.5 (accept 1.45–1.55) (1).","points":[{"text":"Large triangle drawn (at least half the line)","marks":1},{"text":"correct readings of Δ(sin i) and Δ(sin r) from the line","marks":1},{"text":"gradient ⟪=\\Delta (\\sin i)/\\Delta (\\sin r)⟫","marks":1},{"text":"≈ 1.5 (accept 1.45–1.55)","marks":1}],"num":{"value":1.5,"sf":2,"unit":"","unitTex":"","min":1.45,"max":1.55},"label":"(c)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week08.past.q1.d","kind":"self","q":"What does the shape of the graph tell you about the relationship between sin i and sin r? Name the law this verifies.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Straight line through origin (1) → sin i is directly proportional to sin r (1); Snell's law (1).","points":[{"text":"Straight line through origin","marks":1},{"text":"→ sin i is directly proportional to sin r","marks":1},{"text":"Snell's law","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"},{"id":"week08.past.q1.e","kind":"self","q":"State what the gradient represents and use it to calculate the speed of light in the glass.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Gradient = refractive index n (1); ⟪v=c/n=3.0\\times 10^{8}/1.5⟫ (1) ⟪=2.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Gradient = refractive index n","marks":1},{"text":"⟪v=c/n=3.0\\times 10^{8}/1.5⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=2.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"label":"(e)"},{"id":"week08.past.q1.f","kind":"self","q":"State TWO precautions the student should take to obtain accurate results.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Any two: pins vertical and far apart (1); look at the bases of the pins to avoid parallax (1); block kept exactly on its outline (1); sharp pencil / thin lines (1); measure angles from the normal with a protractor accurately (1).","points":[{"text":"Any two: pins vertical and far apart","marks":1},{"text":"look at the bases of the pins to avoid parallax","marks":1},{"text":"block kept exactly on its outline","marks":1},{"text":"sharp pencil / thin lines","marks":1},{"text":"measure angles from the normal with a protractor accurately","marks":1}],"conf":"low","label":"(f)"},{"id":"week08.past.q1.g","kind":"self","q":"Use your graph to find the angle of refraction when ⟪i=45^{\\circ}⟫.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪\\sin 45^{\\circ}=0.71⟫; read ⟪\\sin r\\approx 0.47⟫ from graph (1); ⟪r=\\sin ^{-1}(0.47)\\approx 28^{\\circ}⟫ (accept 27–29°) (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪\\sin 45^{\\circ}=0.71⟫; read ⟪\\sin r\\approx 0.47⟫ from graph","marks":1},{"text":"⟪r=\\sin ^{-1}(0.47)\\approx 28^{\\circ}⟫ (accept 27–29°)","marks":1}],"num":{"value":28,"sf":2,"unit":"°","unitTex":"^{\\circ}","min":27,"max":29},"conf":"low","label":"(g)","autoKind":"num"}]},{"id":"week08.past.q2","kind":"multi","title":"Extended response (Paper 02 Section B style): light in technology","total":15,"stem":["Modern communication and medicine depend on controlling light."],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week08.past.q2.a","kind":"self","q":"Explain, with the aid of a labelled diagram, how light is transmitted along an optical fibre. Include the TWO conditions necessary for the process involved.","marks":5,"level":3,"lines":8,"scheme":"Diagram: fibre with ray zig-zagging by reflection at walls (1); labelled core / cladding (1); total internal reflection (1); condition: light travelling from denser to less dense medium (1); angle of incidence greater than critical angle (1).","points":[{"text":"Diagram: fibre with ray zig-zagging by reflection at walls","marks":1},{"text":"labelled core / cladding","marks":1},{"text":"total internal reflection","marks":1},{"text":"condition: light travelling from denser to less dense medium","marks":1},{"text":"angle of incidence greater than critical angle","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week08.past.q2.b","kind":"self","q":"The core of an optical fibre has a refractive index of 1.52. Calculate (i) the critical angle of the core, (ii) the speed of light in the core.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"(i) ⟪\\sin c=1/1.52=0.658⟫ (1); ⟪c=41.1^{\\circ}⟫ (1). (ii) ⟪v=c/n=3.0\\times 10^{8}/1.52⟫ (1) ⟪=1.97\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"(i) ⟪\\sin c=1/1.52=0.658⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪c=41.1^{\\circ}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"(ii) ⟪v=c/n=3.0\\times 10^{8}/1.52⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=1.97\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week08.past.q2.c","kind":"self","q":"A camera uses a converging lens of focal length 5.0 cm to photograph a person standing 3.0 m away. Calculate the distance between the lens and the film (image sensor), and state the nature of the image.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":5,"scheme":"⟪u=300\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫ (1); ⟪1/v=1/5.0-1/300=0.2000-0.0033=0.1967⟫ (1); ⟪v=5.08\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\approx 5.1\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫ (1); real, inverted, diminished (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪u=300\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪1/v=1/5.0-1/300=0.2000-0.0033=0.1967⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪v=5.08\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\approx 5.1\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"real, inverted, diminished","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week08.past.q2.d","kind":"self","q":"Suggest ONE advantage of using optical fibres instead of copper wires for telephone and internet signals.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Any one with explanation: carry much more information (1) because light has very high frequency / many signals at once (1); OR less signal loss so fewer boosters needed (1)(1); OR no electrical interference / more secure (1)(1).","points":[{"text":"Any one with explanation: carry much more information","marks":1},{"text":"because light has very high frequency / many signals at once","marks":1},{"text":"OR less signal loss so fewer boosters needed","marks":2},{"text":"OR no electrical interference / more secure","marks":2}],"conf":"low","label":"(d)"}]}],"n":62}]};
